#==============================================================================
# zshrc.herdr — the herdr agent-workspace layer
#==============================================================================
# Split out of zshrc.company (DO-555) so herdr can be adopted WITHOUT adopting
# this repo's work-specific configuration. Everything here is conditioned on
# herdr or its plugins; nothing here knows about GitHub accounts, token caches
# or any other Quantivly-specific machinery — that stayed in zshrc.company.
#
# Sourced by zshrc BEFORE zshrc.company, and safe to source on its own:
#
#     source ~/.dotfiles/zsh/zshrc.herdr
#
# That is exactly what `./install --herdr` sets a modular adopter up to do.
#
# Self-contained. hdespawn PREFERS `confirm` (zsh/functions/system.sh) and falls
# back to a `read -q` prompt when it is absent, so a modular adopter who sources
# only this file still gets a confirmation rather than a silent teardown.
# Everything else here shells out to `herdr` / `git` / `jq`.
#
# build-limits lives in zshrc.buildlimits but keys on $HERDR_PANE_ID, so it is
# part of this layer in everything but filename, and it is documented alongside
# these commands. zshrc sources zshrc.buildlimits BEFORE this file, so in a full
# install the guard below is a no-op; it exists so that sourcing this one file
# does not leave a documented command quietly missing.
#
# WHO CALLS WHAT. hspawn / hdespawn / hreap are AGENT-facing: a lead agent
# drives them to fan work out into worktrees and reap it afterwards. A human
# at the keyboard normally uses the herdr UI and `clauth`, not these. The
# `claude` and `herdr-lazy` wrappers below are the reverse — they exist for the
# human's interactive shell.
#==============================================================================

# CLAUDE_PICK_SOURCING is set by scripts/claude-pick, which sources this file to
# reach the picker and wants nothing else from it. Sourcing a SECOND module out
# of a CLI is exactly the top-level side effect the marker exists to keep out —
# and this file has only two such statements, this one and the tenant-file source
# below (which is data the picker needs, so it stays). A state-table row asserts
# that sourcing under the marker emits nothing on either stream and defines no
# build-limits, so a future side effect trips a row rather than the CLI.
if (( ! $+functions[build-limits] )) && [[ -z "${CLAUDE_PICK_SOURCING:-}" ]]; then
  _herdr_layer_dir="${${(%):-%x}:A:h}"
  [[ -f "$_herdr_layer_dir/zshrc.buildlimits" ]] && source "$_herdr_layer_dir/zshrc.buildlimits"
  unset _herdr_layer_dir
fi

#==============================================================================
# Claude Code launcher — herdmates team lead inside Herdr
#==============================================================================
# Inside a Herdr pane, launch through herdmates' teammux shim so the session
# becomes a native Claude Code *team lead*: Claude's split-pane teammate mode
# (teammateMode: tmux) calls what it thinks is tmux, and the shim translates
# every verb into `herdr pane` calls. Teammates then land as first-class,
# steerable Herdr panes instead of hidden in-process subagents.
#
# Native agent teams are still gated behind CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS,
# so it is set here rather than globally — the flag only applies where herdmates
# can actually host the result.
#
# Outside Herdr the shim has no output surface (Claude falls back to in-process
# teammates), so plain claude is used. `command claude` bypasses this entirely.
#
# ACCOUNTS — isolated by default since 2026-09-06:
#   ~/.claude/.credentials.json holds the login AND every plugin MCP token, at
#   0600, with no lock. Measured on this box that day: 17 of 18 live Claude
#   processes had no CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR at all, so they were seventeen holders of
#   one file — and that file belonged to no registered clauth profile, so they
#   were billing an account nobody had selected. The nine login-expiry incidents
#   in the preceding eight days hit 1, 1, 5, 4, 3, 1, 1, 3 and 6 sessions: five of
#   the nine were simultaneous, which is one bad value landing in one shared file
#   taking a whole group down, not holders orphaning each other.
#
#   So a plain `claude` now runs in a persistent per-profile config dir built by
#   scripts/claude-account-dirs.sh — the same layout `clauth start` uses, at a
#   stable path. It keeps the teammux launch below (the dir is just an
#   environment variable, and zsh exports a prefix assignment on a FUNCTION call
#   into that function's children), so isolation no longer costs a team lead.
#
#   `clauth start <profile>` remains the supervised path, with --with-fallback
#   quota rotation; it launches the claude BINARY, so it never reaches this
#   function and cannot lead a team. `clauth <profile>` switches the GLOBAL
#   credential — which under this scheme nothing reads.
#
#   ESCAPE HATCHES, because this must not become an ultimatum:
#     CLAUDE_ISOLATION_OFF=1  — behave exactly as before, on the global file
#     CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_PROFILE  — pin a profile instead of choosing one
#     CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_QUIET=1  — suppress the one-line account announcement
#     `command claude`        — bypasses this function entirely, as it always did
#
#   WITHOUT CLAUTH NOTHING CHANGES. This file is sourced by modular adopters who
#   have no clauth and no ~/.dotfiles/scripts — the whole block is gated on both
#   being present, and the fallback is the previous behaviour byte for byte.

# Where the persistent per-profile config dirs live. Same default as the builder,
# and overridable the same way, so the two cannot disagree.
_claude_account_root() { print -r -- "${CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_DIRS_ROOT:-$HOME/.local/state/claude-account-dirs}"; }
_claude_account_builder() { print -r -- "${DOTFILES_ROOT:-$HOME/.dotfiles}/scripts/claude-account-dirs.sh"; }

# Live holders of one profile's credential file, counted from pidfiles and
# pruned as it counts. `claude()` blocks for the whole session, so the shell's own
# pid is a faithful stand-in for the session; a shell killed without running its
# cleanup leaves an entry that the next count removes. Deliberately NOT read from
# the process table: this runs in an interactive shell, and reading other
# processes' environments to answer a question a file can answer is the wrong
# trade — see "Keeping secrets out of transcripts" in CLAUDE.md.
# Live sessions clauth knows about, as "<profile>\t<pid>" lines.
#
# READ ONLY. These files belong to clauth and a dead entry is ITS to clean up:
# pruning them from here would race clauth's own writer and delete records of
# sessions it is still tracking. That is the opposite of the holders/ pidfiles
# below, which this layer creates and therefore prunes.
#
# `current_member // start_profile`, in that order: a session launched with
# --with-fallback moves between accounts, and start_profile is where it BEGAN,
# not where it is burning now.
_claude_holder_sessions() {
  local d="$HOME/.clauth/live_sessions"
  [[ -d "$d" ]] || return 0
  command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  # The glob is collected FIRST and the empty case returns. An empty expansion
  # gives jq no file operands, and jq then falls back to reading STDIN — where it
  # blocks forever. `(N)` suppresses the "no matches" error but produces exactly
  # that empty expansion, so it converts a missing file into a HANG, not a no-op.
  #
  # Neither hypothetical nor test-only: clauth creates ~/.clauth/live_sessions as
  # a directory, so an empty one is an ordinary resting state — and this function
  # runs on every `claude` launch, which would then never return. Measured:
  # `jq -r . <empty glob>` with stdin open does not terminate (rc 124 under
  # `timeout`). It surfaced as two hung suite processes, having passed earlier
  # runs only because their stdin happened to be at EOF.
  local -a files
  files=( "$d"/*.json(N.) )
  (( ${#files} )) || return 0
  jq -r 'select(type == "object")
         | [ ((.current_member // .start_profile) // empty), (.pid // empty) ]
         | select(length == 2) | @tsv' "${files[@]}" 2>/dev/null
}

# How many live sessions hold this profile, counting BOTH launch paths.
#
# holders/ pidfiles see only claude()/hspawn launches; a `clauth start` session
# — herdr-draft's pinned path — appears only in clauth's live_sessions. Neither
# side alone counts both, and a picker that under-counts hands the next session
# the account that is already busiest.
#
# When CLAUDE_TENANT_BUCKETS groups this profile with others, the count is the
# SUM over the bucket: two logins sharing one rate-limit bucket crowd each other.
# The per-name count still drives the round-robin ledger, so credential holders
# keep spreading across the individual logins.
_claude_holder_count() {
  local name="$1" m
  local -a bucket group
  bucket=( "$name" )
  for group in "${CLAUDE_TENANT_BUCKETS[@]}"; do
    if (( ${${=group}[(Ie)$name]} )); then
      bucket=( ${=group} )
      break
    fi
  done
  local total=0
  for m in "${bucket[@]}"; do
    (( total += $(_claude_holder_count_one "$m") ))
  done
  print -r -- "$total"
}

_claude_holder_count_one() {
  local dir f n=0 line lp lpid
  dir="$(_claude_account_root)/$1/holders"
  if [[ -d "$dir" ]]; then
    for f in "$dir"/*(N.); do
      # These pidfiles are ours, so a dead one is ours to remove.
      if kill -0 "${f:t}" 2>/dev/null; then (( n++ )); else command rm -f -- "$f"; fi
    done
  fi
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r lp lpid; do
    [[ "$lp" == "$1" ]] || continue
    [[ -n "$lpid" ]] || continue
    kill -0 "$lpid" 2>/dev/null && (( n++ ))
  done < <(_claude_holder_sessions)
  print -r -- "$n"
}

# THE POOL. Atrium had a pool of work accounts and a separate personal one; this
# is that, as data. Empty (the default) means "every registered profile", which
# is what a modular adopter with one account wants and what this did before.
#
#   CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_POOL=(quantivly-1 quantivly-2 quantivly-3)
#
# Set it in ~/.zshrc.local. A profile OUTSIDE the pool is never chosen
# automatically — only by name, via `claude-as <profile>`. That is the whole
# point of separating personal from work: a personal account should never be
# spent by a work session that merely happened to start next.
(( ${+CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_POOL} )) || typeset -ga CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_POOL=()

# TENANTS. The pool above answers "which account", but not "which account for
# THIS directory" — so a session started in a personal repository could be billed
# to a work account, and nothing reported it. gh identity and git identity are
# both already routed by repository remote; this makes Claude account selection
# the third, using the SAME rule rather than a second one that can disagree.
#
# The table is DATA and lives outside this repo, exactly as GH_ACCOUNT_ROUTES
# does. Sourced here rather than from ~/.zshrc.local for two reasons: that file
# holds secrets (the secret-emission hook refuses to read it), and it is sourced
# after this one, which would be too late for the functions below.
#
#   CLAUDE_TENANT_ROUTES=( "quantivly=work" )        # owner-glob=tenant
#   CLAUDE_TENANT_PATH_ROUTES=( "$HOME/quantivly=work" )  # only without a remote
#   CLAUDE_TENANT_DEFAULT=personal
#   CLAUDE_TENANT_POOL=( work "quantivly-1 quantivly-2" personal "personal" )
#
# No table loaded is the modular adopter's normal state: the resolver is skipped
# entirely and CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_POOL keeps its exact meaning.
# CLAUDE_TENANT_BUCKETS is a LIST of whitespace-separated groups
# (`( "a b" "c d" )`), not a map — so it is declared -ga with the other lists.
# #129 declared it -gA, copying §5.1, which declares it associative and then
# shows a one-element list as its example value. Those cannot both be right:
#
#     typeset -gA B; B=( "a1 b2" )
#     zsh: bad set of key/value pairs for associative array
#
# The assignment simply fails. It was masked on the machine this was written on
# only because the tenant data file happens to re-declare it -ga first; a tenant
# file that just assigns — which is what §5.1's example does — gets an error and
# an unusable table.
typeset -ga CLAUDE_TENANT_ROUTES CLAUDE_TENANT_PATH_ROUTES CLAUDE_TENANT_BUCKETS
typeset -gA CLAUDE_TENANT_POOL CLAUDE_TENANT_OVERFLOW CLAUDE_TENANT_GH_DIR
: "${CLAUDE_TENANT_DEFAULT=}"
[[ -r "${CLAUDE_TENANTS_FILE:-$HOME/.config/claude-tenants.zsh}" ]] && \
  source "${CLAUDE_TENANTS_FILE:-$HOME/.config/claude-tenants.zsh}"

# Is the WHOLE tenant table usable? Checked before any lookup, so a typo in entry
# three cannot be masked by entry one happening to match — the rule _gh_route_for
# pays for, and the unparseable-link-map failure the live-config guard pays for,
# in a third place.
#
# Returns 1 for BOTH "no table" and "bad table". They are different states with
# different fixes, and _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE says which: `none` falls back to
# CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_POOL semantics, `bad-table` is loud and selects nothing.
_claude_tenant_table_ok() {
  local entry t m ppfx
  _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY=""
  if (( ! ${#CLAUDE_TENANT_ROUTES} && ! ${#CLAUDE_TENANT_PATH_ROUTES} \
        && ! ${#CLAUDE_TENANT_POOL} )) && [[ -z "$CLAUDE_TENANT_DEFAULT" ]]; then
    _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=none
    return 1
  fi
  for entry in "${CLAUDE_TENANT_ROUTES[@]}"; do
    if [[ "$entry" != *=* || -z "${entry%%=*}" || -z "${entry#*=}" ]]; then
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=bad-table
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="CLAUDE_TENANT_ROUTES entry is not 'owner-pattern=tenant': '$entry'"
      return 1
    fi
    # ${~pat} enables tilde expansion as well as globbing, so a pattern starting
    # with ~ aborts the lookup with "no such user or named directory" and takes
    # every later route with it. Same restriction, for the same reason, as
    # _gh_route_for's owner patterns.
    if [[ -n "${${entry%%=*}//[A-Za-z0-9_.?*-]/}" ]]; then
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=bad-table
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="CLAUDE_TENANT_ROUTES owner pattern may only contain letters, digits, - _ . and the * ? wildcards: '${entry%%=*}'"
      return 1
    fi
  done
  for entry in "${CLAUDE_TENANT_PATH_ROUTES[@]}"; do
    if [[ "$entry" != *=* || -z "${entry%%=*}" || -z "${entry#*=}" ]]; then
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=bad-table
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="CLAUDE_TENANT_PATH_ROUTES entry is not 'absolute-prefix=tenant': '$entry'"
      return 1
    fi
    ppfx="${entry%%=*}"
    if [[ "$ppfx" == '~' || "$ppfx" == '~/'* ]]; then
      ppfx="${HOME}${ppfx#\~}"
    elif [[ "$ppfx" == '~'* ]]; then
      # ~user is a named directory this expansion cannot honour; splicing $HOME
      # in front of it yields an absolute garbage path that passes the check
      # below and then silently never matches.
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=bad-table
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="CLAUDE_TENANT_PATH_ROUTES prefix '${entry%%=*}' is a named directory (~user); write the absolute path"
      return 1
    fi
    if [[ "$ppfx" != /* ]]; then
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=bad-table
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="CLAUDE_TENANT_PATH_ROUTES prefix must be an absolute path (a leading ~/ is expanded): '${entry%%=*}'"
      return 1
    fi
  done
  # Every tenant a route, the default, or an overflow key names must have a pool.
  # A tenant with no pool selects an EMPTY set, and an empty set is
  # indistinguishable from "no pool configured" — which means EVERY profile. So a
  # typo here would silently WIDEN the pool at the moment it looks narrowed.
  for t in "${(@)${(@)CLAUDE_TENANT_ROUTES#*=}}" "${(@)${(@)CLAUDE_TENANT_PATH_ROUTES#*=}}" \
           "${(@k)CLAUDE_TENANT_OVERFLOW}" $CLAUDE_TENANT_DEFAULT; do
    [[ -n "$t" ]] || continue
    if [[ -z "${CLAUDE_TENANT_POOL[$t]-}" ]]; then
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=bad-table
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="tenant '$t' is named by a route, the default or an overflow entry but has no CLAUDE_TENANT_POOL entry"
      return 1
    fi
  done
  # Pool values are whitespace-split, so a profile name needing whitespace cannot
  # be expressed at all; anything else illegal is named here rather than left to
  # quietly match no profile later.
  for t in "${(@v)CLAUDE_TENANT_POOL}" "${(@v)CLAUDE_TENANT_OVERFLOW}"; do
    for m in ${=t}; do
      if [[ -n "${m//[A-Za-z0-9_.-]/}" ]]; then
        _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=bad-table
        _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="pool member '$m' may only contain letters, digits, - _ and ."
        return 1
      fi
    done
  done
  return 0
}

# Which tenant owns a directory? Remote first, path second, default last — the
# rule _gh_route_for applies, REUSED rather than reimplemented. Copying
# _gh_url_owner here would give the machine two answers to "who owns this repo",
# and the whole point of this layer is that gh, git and Claude give one.
#
# Usage: _claude_tenant_for [dir]
# Sets:  _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE = matched | default | none | bad-table
#                             | git-error | unsupported
#        _CLAUDE_TENANT, _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY
# Returns 0 when _CLAUDE_TENANT is usable.
_claude_tenant_for() {
  local dir="${1:-$PWD}" entry pat ppfx ppfx_abs dir_abs t i
  local -a _GH_REMOTE_NAMES _GH_REMOTE_URLS _GH_REMOTE_OWNERS _GH_REMOTE_KINDS
  local _GH_REMOTE_STATE _GH_REMOTE_ERR
  local has_github_remote=0 why_no_route=""
  _CLAUDE_TENANT=""; _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY=""; _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=""

  _claude_tenant_table_ok || return 1        # leaves state none | bad-table

  # A modular adopter has zshrc.herdr and no github.sh, so the owner half of the
  # table cannot be evaluated at all. That is a MESSAGE, not a second URL parser
  # — duplicating _gh_url_owner here is exactly the drift this design avoids.
  # Path routes and the default still work.
  if (( ! $+functions[_gh_repo_remotes] )); then
    if (( ${#CLAUDE_TENANT_ROUTES} )); then
      # Once per SHELL, not once per call: this fires from a chpwd-adjacent path
      # and a line printed on every cd is a line nobody reads.
      if [[ -z "${_CLAUDE_TENANT_WARNED_UNSUPPORTED:-}" ]]; then
        typeset -g _CLAUDE_TENANT_WARNED_UNSUPPORTED=1
        print -u2 -- "claude: CLAUDE_TENANT_ROUTES needs zsh/functions/github.sh, which is not loaded — owner routes are being skipped; path routes and the default still apply."
      fi
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=unsupported
    fi
    why_no_route="owner routes unavailable (github.sh not loaded)"
  else
    _gh_repo_remotes "$dir"
    # "git could not be asked" is NOT "there is nothing here". Falling through to
    # the default would hand a work repository the personal pool on the one path
    # that deliberately stays quiet — the silent-wrong-account shape this whole
    # layer exists to end.
    if [[ "$_GH_REMOTE_STATE" == "git-error" ]]; then
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=git-error
      _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="git could not read '$dir' — the remote is UNKNOWN, so no tenant was selected: ${_GH_REMOTE_ERR:-(no message)}"
      return 1
    fi
    case "$_GH_REMOTE_STATE" in
      no-repo)    why_no_route="not a git repository — no remote to route on" ;;
      no-remotes) why_no_route="git repository with no remotes — nothing to route on" ;;
      *)          why_no_route="no route matched any remote" ;;
    esac
    for entry in "${CLAUDE_TENANT_ROUTES[@]}"; do
      pat="${entry%%=*}"; t="${entry#*=}"
      for (( i = 1; i <= ${#_GH_REMOTE_OWNERS[@]}; i++ )); do
        [[ -n "${_GH_REMOTE_OWNERS[i]}" ]] || continue
        if [[ "${_GH_REMOTE_OWNERS[i]:l}" == ${~pat:l} ]]; then
          _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=matched
          _CLAUDE_TENANT="$t"
          _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="remote '${_GH_REMOTE_NAMES[i]}' owner '${_GH_REMOTE_OWNERS[i]}' matches route '$pat'"
          return 0
        fi
      done
    done
    for (( i = 1; i <= ${#_GH_REMOTE_KINDS[@]}; i++ )); do
      case "${_GH_REMOTE_KINDS[i]}" in
        github) has_github_remote=1; break ;;
        unparsable)
          # A GitHub URL nobody can read is still a GitHub remote: it blocks the
          # path table and the default answers. The same rule as #127's, so gh
          # and Claude cannot disagree about the same directory.
          has_github_remote=1
          why_no_route="remote '${_GH_REMOTE_NAMES[i]}' looks like GitHub but its URL could not be parsed — not routed by path"
          break ;;
      esac
    done
  fi

  # Only a directory with no GitHub remote may be decided by its PLACE. A remote
  # that matched no route has already answered "the default tenant" — the answer
  # git identity gives — and the path table must not overrule it.
  if (( ! has_github_remote )); then
    dir_abs="${dir:A}"
    for entry in "${CLAUDE_TENANT_PATH_ROUTES[@]}"; do
      ppfx="${entry%%=*}"; t="${entry#*=}"
      [[ "$ppfx" == '~'* ]] && ppfx="${HOME}${ppfx#\~}"
      ppfx_abs="${ppfx:A}"
      # Component match, both sides resolved through symlinks: ~/quantivly-other
      # is not under ~/quantivly, and a symlinked checkout still lands inside.
      if [[ "$dir_abs" == "$ppfx_abs" || "$dir_abs" == "$ppfx_abs"/* ]]; then
        # `unsupported` survives a path match for the same reason it survives the
        # default: an owner route might have matched FIRST and chosen a different
        # tenant, and the caller cannot tell a complete answer from one produced
        # with half the table unreadable unless this says so.
        [[ "$_CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE" == unsupported ]] || _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=matched
        _CLAUDE_TENANT="$t"
        _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="$why_no_route — under path route '${entry%%=*}'"
        return 0
      fi
    done
  fi

  if [[ -n "$CLAUDE_TENANT_DEFAULT" ]]; then
    # `unsupported` survives: the caller must be able to tell a complete answer
    # from one produced with half the table unreadable.
    [[ "$_CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE" == unsupported ]] || _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=default
    _CLAUDE_TENANT="$CLAUDE_TENANT_DEFAULT"
    _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="$why_no_route — CLAUDE_TENANT_DEFAULT"
    return 0
  fi
  _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE=none
  _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY="$why_no_route, and CLAUDE_TENANT_DEFAULT is unset"
  return 1
}

# Derive the gh routing tables from the tenant table, so adding a root is ONE
# edit rather than two that can disagree. Additive and idempotent: it never
# removes or reorders a team default.
#
# Defined here, deliberately NOT called here. Load order is zshrc.herdr (this
# file) -> zshrc.company, which ASSIGNS GH_ACCOUNT_ROUTES=(…) wholesale and would
# discard anything added first -> ~/.zshrc.local -> the machine's own overlay,
# which calls this as its last line. Only that caller knows the order has ended.
claude-tenants-apply-gh() {
  local e t d
  for e in "${CLAUDE_TENANT_ROUTES[@]}"; do
    t="${e#*=}"; d="${CLAUDE_TENANT_GH_DIR[$t]:-}"; [[ -n "$d" ]] || continue
    (( ${GH_ACCOUNT_ROUTES[(I)${e%%=*}=*]} )) || GH_ACCOUNT_ROUTES+=("${e%%=*}=$d")
  done
  for e in "${CLAUDE_TENANT_PATH_ROUTES[@]}"; do
    t="${e#*=}"; d="${CLAUDE_TENANT_GH_DIR[$t]:-}"; [[ -n "$d" ]] || continue
    (( ${GH_ACCOUNT_PATH_ROUTES[(I)${e%%=*}=*]} )) || GH_ACCOUNT_PATH_ROUTES+=("${e%%=*}=$d")
  done
  [[ -n "${GH_ACCOUNT_DEFAULT_DIR:-}" ]] || \
    GH_ACCOUNT_DEFAULT_DIR="${CLAUDE_TENANT_GH_DIR[$CLAUDE_TENANT_DEFAULT]:-}"
}

# Is this profile excluded from automatic selection, and why? Prints a reason, or
# nothing when the profile is selectable.
#
# Both states below are ones clauth writes and nothing here can repair. Picking a
# quarantined account is not a neutral mistake: `personal` was auth_broken on
# 2026-09-08 and the old holder-count picker chose it *because* nothing was
# running on it — the emptiness that quarantine causes read as headroom.
_claude_profile_excluded() {
  local name="$1" cfg="$HOME/.clauth/profiles/$1/config.toml"
  local toml="$HOME/.clauth/profiles.toml"
  # `disabled = true`, ignoring commented-out template lines.
  if [[ -r "$cfg" ]] && command grep -qE '^[[:space:]]*disabled[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*true' "$cfg"; then
    print -r -- "disabled"; return 0
  fi
  # auth_broken = ["a", "b"] — clauth's quarantine list, which it writes itself.
  #
  # A sed RANGE, not a single line: clauth already writes `profiles = [` across
  # several lines in this same file, with the same writer and the same array
  # type, so a line-based match is one quarantined account away from silently
  # seeing nothing — and then the quarantined profile reads as maximum headroom,
  # which is the bug this exclusion exists to prevent. Same shape CLAUDE.md
  # records for `fallback_chain` in claude-doctor. Bounded by construction:
  # anchored at the assignment, quits at the first `]`.
  if [[ -r "$toml" ]]; then
    local span
    span="$(command sed -n '/^[[:space:]]*auth_broken[[:space:]]*=/,/]/{p; /]/q}' "$toml" 2>/dev/null)"
    if [[ -n "$span" && "$span" == *"\"$name\""* ]]; then
      print -r -- "auth broken — clauth login $name"; return 0
    fi
  fi
  return 1
}

# _claude_profile_load WAS HERE, and it is gone. It answered "the worse of this
# profile's 5h and 7d utilization" — the whole of the pre-DO-574 ranking — and
# _claude_pick_for_dir asks _claude_profile_metrics for the two figures
# separately instead, because the score needs them apart (weekly headroom is a
# multiplier, not a co-equal maximum) and because "the worse of the two" cannot
# express a window that is about to reset. Nothing called it after the picker was
# rewritten, and a dead function beside a live one that reads the same file is an
# invitation to reintroduce the old ranking by accident. `CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_CACHE_MAX_AGE`
# went with it; the threshold is `CLAUDE_PICK_CACHE_MAX_AGE`.

# Seconds since a profile's usage cache was written, or nothing.
#
# Loads its own modules. A helper that depends on a module its CALLER happened to
# load returns nothing when called any other way — and "no age" reads as "fresh",
# so a stale reading would have been believed.
_claude_profile_cache_age() {
  local f="$HOME/.clauth/profiles/$1/usage_cache.json" mtime
  [[ -r "$f" ]] || return 1
  zmodload -F zsh/stat b:zstat 2>/dev/null || return 1
  zmodload zsh/datetime 2>/dev/null || return 1
  mtime=$(zstat +mtime "$f" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  print -r -- $(( EPOCHSECONDS - mtime ))
}

# Seconds from now until an RFC 3339 instant, into _CLAUDE_TS_DELTA. `unknown`
# for an absent or unparseable one, and NEVER a number — reading a missing reset
# as 0 puts a fresh window at maximum "about to reset" urgency, the precise
# opposite of the truth.
#
# `strftime -r -s VAR`, not `$(strftime -r ...)`: the -s form assigns without a
# fork, and this runs twice per pool member on every `claude` launch.
#
# One parser for both the 5h and the weekly instant. Two copies would be two
# places for the trimming, the zone and the parse-failure guard to be got wrong
# independently, and the guard is the whole function: without it a failed parse
# leaves a stale value that reads as a measurement.
#
# THE ZONE IS NOT DECORATION, and reading it as one cost three hours. `strftime
# -r` goes through strptime and then mktime, and **mktime interprets a
# broken-down time in LOCAL time and ignores any offset strptime parsed** — so
# simply cutting `2099-01-01T00:00:00.000000+00:00` at its first `.` and parsing
# the rest yields an instant wrong by this machine's own UTC offset. Measured
# 2026-09-10 in IST (UTC+2): that input parsed to 4070901600, two hours before
# the 4070908800 it names. The cost is not cosmetic — r5 drives the
# use-it-or-lose-it bonus over an 18000 s window, so a 3 h summer offset is 60%
# of the window, and it shifts every reset time the §5.4 messages print, which is
# the number a reader uses to decide how long to wait. `local TZ=UTC` is scoped
# to this function (checked: TZ is unset again on return), and the zone the
# string carries is applied arithmetically afterwards rather than through
# strptime's `%z`, which mktime would discard exactly as it discards the rest.
#
# A string with NO zone at all is read as UTC and said so here, because the only
# other reading imports the reader's own timezone into a number that is then
# compared against other machines' — clauth has always written an offset.
_claude_ts_delta() {
  local s="${1:-}" body="" zone="" secs off=0 TZ=UTC
  _CLAUDE_TS_DELTA=unknown
  [[ -n "$s" ]] || return 1
  zmodload zsh/datetime 2>/dev/null || return 1

  body="$s"
  case "$s" in
    *[Zz])
      # DEFENCE IN DEPTH, AND UNPINNABLE — written down because a mutant that
      # can never die reads as coverage. Measured here: glibc's strptime IGNORES
      # trailing input, so leaving the `Z` on the string parses identically and a
      # mutant removing this arm is behaviourally identical. It stays because the
      # function's contract is "split the instant from its zone, then apply the
      # zone", and an implementation that only works because strptime is lax is
      # one strict strptime away from returning `unknown` for every UTC instant.
      body="${s%?}" ;;
    *[+-]<->:<->)
      zone="${s: -6}"; body="${s%??????}" ;;
    *[0-9][+-]<->)
      zone="${s: -5}"; body="${s%?????}" ;;
  esac
  if [[ -n "$zone" ]]; then
    # ±hh:mm or ±hhmm. Seconds EAST of UTC, so it is SUBTRACTED to get UTC.
    local zh="${${zone:1}%%:*}" zm="${${zone:1}##*:}"
    [[ "$zone" == ?<->:<-> ]] || zm="${${zone:1}: -2}"
    off=$(( 10#$zh * 3600 + 10#$zm * 60 ))
    [[ "${zone:0:1}" == - ]] && off=$(( - off ))
  fi
  strftime -r -s secs '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S' "${body%%.*}" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  [[ -n "$secs" ]] || return 1
  _CLAUDE_TS_DELTA=$(( secs - off - EPOCHSECONDS ))
  return 0
}

# One profile's usage numbers. Every field is either an integer or the literal
# string `unknown` — there is no third answer, and no field silently becomes 0.
#
# Usage: _claude_profile_metrics <profile>
# Sets:  _CPM_U5   5h utilization, 0-100
#        _CPM_R5   seconds until the 5h window resets (negative = already rolled)
#        _CPM_UW   worst of seven_day and every weekly_scoped window
#        _CPM_RW   seconds until the AGGREGATE weekly window resets
#        _CPM_AGE  seconds since the cache was written
#        _CPM_TIER plan tier, informational only — NEVER a ranking input
#
# ABSENT IS ITS OWN STATE, and this is measured, not defensive: on 2026-09-10
# three of five profiles here had `five_hour: {"utilization": 0.0}` with **no
# resets_at key at all** — absent exactly where utilization is 0, i.e. a window
# that has not started yet. Reading a missing reset as 0 would put every fresh
# window at maximum "about to reset" urgency, which is the precise opposite of
# the truth, and would hand it the largest use-it-or-lose-it bonus in the pool.
#
# _CPM_RW is the SEVEN_DAY window's own reset, and it is labelled that way rather
# than as "the reset of the window uW names": uW may come from a per-model
# weekly_scoped window, each of which carries its own instant, so claiming one
# reset for the worst of them would be an invented answer. The aggregate week is
# what clauth's own status line reports, and it is the one this publishes.
_claude_profile_metrics() {
  local f="$HOME/.clauth/profiles/$1/usage_cache.json" line u5 rs uw rws tier
  local _CLAUDE_TS_DELTA
  _CPM_U5=unknown; _CPM_R5=unknown; _CPM_UW=unknown; _CPM_RW=unknown
  _CPM_AGE=unknown; _CPM_TIER=unknown
  [[ -r "$f" ]] || return 1
  command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  zmodload zsh/datetime 2>/dev/null

  # One jq fork per profile, emitting a TSV line. Never `// 0`: a missing number
  # and a measured zero are different answers, and this whole function exists to
  # keep them apart.
  #
  # `// null`, NOT `// empty`, for the positional fields. Inside `[ ... ]` an
  # `empty` produces NO element rather than a blank one, so every absent field
  # SHIFTS the remaining ones left and they are read into the wrong variables.
  # Measured while writing the rows: a profile with no `resets_at` put its weekly
  # figure in the reset slot and left uW `unknown`; a profile with no `five_hour`
  # block at all reported its seven_day number as the 5h utilization. `null`
  # keeps the column and `@tsv` renders it as the empty string.
  # (`empty` is still correct INSIDE the weekly array, where dropping a
  # non-number is exactly what is wanted before `max`.)
  #
  # The tier is an OBJECT on a Max plan and a STRING on Team (measured:
  # `{"Max":20}` vs `"Team"`), so it is normalised to its key rather than
  # tostring'd into a JSON blob nobody can read.
  line="$(jq -r '
      [ (.five_hour.utilization // null | if . == null then null else floor end),
        (.five_hour.resets_at   // null),
        ([ (.seven_day.utilization // empty),
           ((.weekly_scoped // []) | .[]? | .utilization // empty) ]
           | map(select(type == "number"))
           | if length > 0 then (max | floor) else null end),
        (.seven_day.resets_at // null),
        (.plan.tier // null
           | if type == "object" then (keys | .[0])
             elif . == null then null
             else tostring end)
      ] | @tsv' "$f" 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  [[ -n "$line" ]] || return 1

  # `${(@ps:\t:)line}`, NOT `IFS=$'\t' read`. TAB is an IFS *whitespace*
  # character, so `read` folds a run of them into ONE delimiter and drops
  # trailing empties — which shifts every field after an absent one into the
  # wrong variable. That is the same defect as the `// empty` one above, one
  # layer down, and fixing the jq alone left it fully intact: measured, a profile
  # with no resets_at still read its weekly figure as the reset. This split
  # preserves empty columns.
  local -a _cpm
  _cpm=( "${(@ps:\t:)line}" )
  u5="${_cpm[1]}"; rs="${_cpm[2]}"; uw="${_cpm[3]}"; rws="${_cpm[4]}"; tier="${_cpm[5]}"
  [[ -n "$u5"   ]] && _CPM_U5="$u5"
  [[ -n "$uw"   ]] && _CPM_UW="$uw"
  [[ -n "$tier" ]] && _CPM_TIER="$tier"

  # Both instants through the one parser above, which leaves `unknown` on any
  # failure. A parse failure must never leave a number.
  _claude_ts_delta "$rs"  && _CPM_R5="$_CLAUDE_TS_DELTA"
  _claude_ts_delta "$rws" && _CPM_RW="$_CLAUDE_TS_DELTA"
  _CPM_AGE="$(_claude_profile_cache_age "$1")" || _CPM_AGE=unknown
  return 0
}

# The score for one candidate, in centipoints. Higher is better; may be negative.
#
# Usage: _claude_pick_score <u5> <r5> <uW> <holders>
#   r5 and uW accept the literal `unknown`.
#
# INTEGER ARITHMETIC ONLY — no zsh/mathfunc. The existing picker is integer-only,
# the state tables compare exact strings, and the from-scratch test PATH cannot
# vouch for a module.
#
#   base  = 5h headroom. The backbone: how much of this window is left.
#   bonus = use-it-or-lose-it. Scales with BOTH headroom and closeness to the
#           reset, so a nearly-spent window resetting soon earns little and a
#           fresh window earns nothing extra.
#   weekf = weekly headroom as a MULTIPLIER, never a gate. This is the deliberate
#           departure from the spec, and it is measured rather than argued: two
#           Team seats here read seven_day 100 with live sessions on them, there
#           were ZERO weekly-reset refusals in 750 transcripts over 7 days, and
#           the block a Team seat actually hits is "You've hit your individual
#           spend limit — ask your admin", whose remedy is a person, not a window
#           rolling over. Refusing locally never makes such an account usable
#           sooner, and it would have taken out the entire work pool at once.
#   crowd = holders, costing more as the account fills. W_HOLDER alone spreads
#           sessions for rotation-race safety; u5*W_CROWD makes each additional
#           holder dearer on an account that is already busy.
_claude_pick_score() {
  local u5="$1" r5="$2" uw="$3" h="${4:-0}"
  local w_expire=${CLAUDE_PICK_W_EXPIRE:-50} w_holder=${CLAUDE_PICK_W_HOLDER:-3}
  local w_crowd=${CLAUDE_PICK_W_CROWD:-15} week_low=${CLAUDE_PICK_WEEK_LOW:-15}
  local h5 hw exp weekf base bonus crowd

  # An unknown 5h reading ranks as fully spent HERE, but the caller has already
  # classed it `unknown` and sorts it after every measured candidate — this is
  # only so the arithmetic below has a number to work with.
  [[ "$u5" == unknown ]] && u5=100
  h5=$(( 100 - u5 ))
  (( h5 < 0 )) && h5=0

  if [[ "$uw" == unknown ]]; then
    # Unknown must not outrank a measured value, but neither may it INVENT a
    # penalty: an unread weekly window is not a spent one. Class handles rank.
    weekf=100
  else
    hw=$(( 100 - uw ))
    (( hw < 0 )) && hw=0
    if (( hw >= week_low )); then weekf=100; else weekf=$(( hw * 100 / week_low )); fi
  fi

  # UNKNOWN r5 earns NO bonus — the same as a window nowhere near its reset.
  # Measured: resets_at is absent exactly when the window has not started, so
  # "no reset instant" means "the whole window is ahead", which is precisely the
  # case with nothing to use or lose. It is also the conservative reading.
  #
  # A NEGATIVE r5 earns no bonus either, and this departs from §5.3's arithmetic
  # deliberately. `100 - min(100, max(0,r5)*100/18000)` yields 100 — the MAXIMUM
  # use-it-or-lose-it urgency — for a window that has already reset, which is
  # backwards: there is nothing left to lose, the window is fresh. The spec
  # reached that value only because it routed r5 < 0 through a "post-reset"
  # class that re-fetched the cache first, and no such refresh exists (there is
  # no fetch entry point in clauth outside the TUI and the daemon). Without it
  # the cached u5 belongs to the PREVIOUS window and is therefore an upper bound
  # on the true one — pessimistic, which is safe — so the reading is kept and
  # only the bonus is dropped. Treating the whole profile as `unknown` instead
  # would rank it after every measured candidate, and on this machine the
  # account with the most headroom by far is one whose window had just rolled.
  if [[ "$r5" == unknown ]] || (( r5 >= 18000 )) || (( r5 <= 0 )); then
    exp=0
  else
    exp=$(( 100 - (r5 * 100 / 18000) ))
  fi

  base=$(( h5 * 100 ))
  bonus=$(( h5 * exp * w_expire / 100 ))
  crowd=$(( h * (w_holder * 100 + u5 * w_crowd) ))
  print -r -- $(( (base + bonus) * weekf / 100 - crowd ))
}

# Which class is this profile in, and why? Prints `eligible`, or `<class>:<why>`.
#
# Usage: _claude_pick_class <profile>
# Classes: excluded  — disabled, auth_broken, or no credential. Never chosen.
#          exhausted — the 5h window is spent. A real, self-clearing wall.
#          unknown   — no cache, or one too old to believe. Ranks after every
#                      measured candidate; unknown never outranks a measurement.
#          eligible  — scored.
#
# THE WEEKLY WINDOW IS NOT AN EXHAUSTION CLASS, and that is a deliberate
# departure from §5.3, measured rather than argued. §5.3 listed
# `uW >= WEEK_EXHAUSTED (100)` as exhausted, which every headless caller refuses
# on. Measured on this machine 2026-09-10:
#
#   * two Team seats read seven_day 100 WITH LIVE SESSIONS ON THEM;
#   * ZERO "usage limit reached" / weekly-reset refusals across 750 transcripts
#     over 7 days — the refusal that class was modelled on never fired once;
#   * what a Team seat actually hits is "You've hit your individual spend limit ·
#     run /usage-credits to ask your admin for a higher limit" (34 in 24 h),
#     whose remedy is a PERSON, not a window rolling over, so refusing locally
#     never makes such an account usable any sooner;
#   * their per-model weekly_scoped windows read 38 and 54 — the block is
#     partial, which is why work continues on them;
#   * clauth itself calls its weekly line a SWITCH line (default 98, "aggregate
#     and per-model"), governing auto-switching rather than availability.
#
# With all three work accounts at 100 that morning, a hard block would have made
# hspawn and herdr-draft refuse across the whole work tree while work was
# demonstrably possible. So the weekly window DEMOTES instead: `weekf` in the
# score collapses such an account to ~0, ranking it below anything with headroom
# while leaving it choosable when it is all there is.
#
# CLAUDE_PICK_WEEK_EXHAUSTED stays as an INERT knob, defaulting to 101 so it can
# never fire, for whoever wants the strict behaviour back.
_claude_pick_class() {
  local name="$1" why
  local ex5=${CLAUDE_PICK_5H_EXHAUSTED:-97}
  local exw=${CLAUDE_PICK_WEEK_EXHAUSTED:-101}
  local max_age=${CLAUDE_PICK_CACHE_MAX_AGE:-3600}
  local _CPM_U5 _CPM_R5 _CPM_UW _CPM_AGE _CPM_TIER

  # A DIRECTORY under profiles/ is not a profile. clauth leaves runtime dirs and
  # a .reconcile.lock behind under a name it no longer registers (observed
  # 2026-09-10), and the result looks exactly like a profile to any glob.
  if [[ ! -e "$HOME/.clauth/profiles/$name/credentials.json" \
     && ! -L "$HOME/.clauth/profiles/$name/credentials.json" ]]; then
    print -r -- "excluded:no credential"
    return 0
  fi
  if why="$(_claude_profile_excluded "$name")"; then
    print -r -- "excluded:$why"
    return 0
  fi

  _claude_profile_metrics "$name" >/dev/null 2>&1
  if [[ "$_CPM_U5" == unknown ]]; then
    print -r -- "unknown:no usage reading"
    return 0
  fi
  # Stale is unknown. The threshold is 3600 and STAYS there: there is no
  # refresher to keep caches fresher than that — clauth's only usage writer is
  # gated on a lease taken by the TUI and the daemon alone — so §5.3's proposed
  # 900 would mark every profile unknown for most of the day and leave the picker
  # ranking on nothing at all.
  if [[ "$_CPM_AGE" != unknown ]] && (( _CPM_AGE > max_age )); then
    print -r -- "unknown:usage reading is ${_CPM_AGE}s old"
    return 0
  fi
  if (( _CPM_U5 >= ex5 )); then
    print -r -- "exhausted:5h window ${_CPM_U5}% used"
    return 0
  fi
  if [[ "$_CPM_UW" != unknown ]] && (( _CPM_UW >= exw )); then
    print -r -- "exhausted:weekly window ${_CPM_UW}% used"
    return 0
  fi
  print -r -- "eligible"
}

# --- round-robin ledger -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Scores separate accounts that differ MEANINGFULLY. Within CLAUDE_PICK_RR_BAND
# the difference is noise, and picking the same account every time would pile
# every session onto it — the concentration this whole layer exists to undo. So
# inside the band the tie breaks by LEAST RECENTLY PICKED, and the ledger is what
# remembers that.
#
# One central file, not one per profile: the decision is "which of these", and a
# single lock around read -> decide -> write closes the race two shells starting
# in the same second would otherwise hit. A deterministic sort cannot: both would
# read the same "least recent" and both would choose it.

_claude_pick_ledger_file() { print -r -- "$(_claude_account_root)/.pick-ledger"; }
_claude_pick_ledger_lock() { print -r -- "$(_claude_account_root)/.pick-ledger.lock"; }

# profile -> epoch of its last pick, into the assoc _CLAUDE_LEDGER.
_claude_pick_ledger_read() {
  local f line p t
  typeset -gA _CLAUDE_LEDGER
  _CLAUDE_LEDGER=()
  f="$(_claude_pick_ledger_file)"
  [[ -r "$f" ]] || return 0
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r p t; do
    [[ -n "$p" && -n "$t" ]] || continue
    _CLAUDE_LEDGER[$p]="$t"
  done < "$f"
  return 0
}

# Record a pick. Temp-and-rename IN THE SAME DIRECTORY, so a reader either sees
# the old file or the new one and never a half-written one — a plain `>` would
# truncate the ledger for as long as the write takes, and a concurrent reader
# would see every account as never-picked and pile onto one.
_claude_pick_ledger_write() {
  local name="$1" f tmp p
  f="$(_claude_pick_ledger_file)"
  [[ -d "${f:h}" ]] || command mkdir -p "${f:h}" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  _claude_pick_ledger_read
  zmodload zsh/datetime 2>/dev/null
  _CLAUDE_LEDGER[$name]="$EPOCHSECONDS"
  tmp="${f:h}/.${f:t}.$$"
  {
    # `$'\t'` spliced in, NOT "\t" inside the string: `print -r` is precisely the
    # flag that stops escape expansion, so "$p\t$v" writes a literal backslash-t.
    # The reader then finds no tab, splits nothing, and every entry is discarded
    # — the ledger looks written (right size, new inode) and reads back empty, so
    # round-robin silently degrades to "always the top score".
    for p in "${(@k)_CLAUDE_LEDGER}"; do
      print -r -- "$p"$'\t'"${_CLAUDE_LEDGER[$p]}"
    done
  } > "$tmp" 2>/dev/null || { command rm -f -- "$tmp"; return 1 }
  command mv -f -- "$tmp" "$f" 2>/dev/null || { command rm -f -- "$tmp"; return 1 }
  return 0
}

# Run a snippet holding the ledger lock, or WITHOUT it after a bounded wait.
#
# A stuck lock must never block a launch: the worst case of proceeding unlocked
# is two sessions choosing the same account in the same second, which is the
# ordinary pre-ledger behaviour. The worst case of blocking is that `claude`
# hangs. So the timeout is short, the fallback is to proceed, and it SAYS so —
# an unlocked pick that stayed silent would make the ledger look reliable when
# it is not.
_claude_pick_with_ledger_lock() {
  local fd lock
  lock="$(_claude_pick_ledger_lock)"
  [[ -d "${lock:h}" ]] || command mkdir -p "${lock:h}" 2>/dev/null
  # `zsystem flock` opens the file WITHOUT O_CREAT, so a lock file that does not
  # exist yet is not an unlocked lock — it is an open failure
  # ("failed to open ...: no such file or directory"), which lands in the
  # proceed-unlocked fallback below. And since nothing else ever creates that
  # path, it lands there FOREVER: measured 2026-09-10, every pick on a fresh
  # machine warned "could not take the pick ledger lock" and serialised nothing.
  # The row that covered this asserted the WARNING, which the broken state
  # produces too, so it passed throughout — and its own background holder failed
  # to open the same missing file, so nothing was ever actually held.
  # `: >>` creates without truncating, so it cannot clear a lock in use.
  [[ -e "$lock" ]] || : >>"$lock" 2>/dev/null
  zmodload zsh/system 2>/dev/null || { "$@"; return $?; }
  if zsystem flock -t "${CLAUDE_PICK_LOCK_WAIT:-2}" -f fd "$lock" 2>/dev/null; then
    "$@"
    local rc=$?
    zsystem flock -u "$fd" 2>/dev/null
    return $rc
  fi
  _claude_pick_warnings+=("could not take the pick ledger lock within ${CLAUDE_PICK_LOCK_WAIT:-2}s — proceeding unlocked, so two launches in the same second may choose the same account")
  "$@"
}

# Choose from scored candidates. Input: the array _claude_pick_cands, each entry
# "<score>\t<profile>". Sets REPLY.
#
# Best score wins, EXCEPT that everything within RR_BAND of it is treated as
# tied, and among the tied the least recently picked wins — then name, so the
# result is deterministic when the ledger says nothing.
_claude_pick_choose() {
  local band=${CLAUDE_PICK_RR_BAND:-800}
  local e s n best="" bestscore="" cand_t cand_n pick="" pickt=""
  local -a byname
  (( ${#_claude_pick_cands} )) || return 1

  for e in "${_claude_pick_cands[@]}"; do
    s="${e%%$'\t'*}"
    if [[ -z "$bestscore" ]] || (( s > bestscore )); then bestscore="$s"; fi
  done

  _claude_pick_ledger_read
  # "Then name" has to be built, not inherited from the input's own sort. The
  # entries are "<score>\t<name>" strings, so `${(o)}` over them sorts by the
  # SCORE TEXT: with an empty ledger every candidate's time is 0, `<` is never
  # true, and the first in iteration order wins — which inside the band made the
  # LOWEST-scoring member win (measured: 9000/a1 against 8500/b2 chose b2 with no
  # ledger at all), and made a negative score sort ahead of every positive one.
  # Flipping the fields for the sort alone makes the documented rule the real one
  # and leaves the input format the rows are written against untouched.
  for e in "${_claude_pick_cands[@]}"; do
    byname+=( "${e#*$'\t'}"$'\t'"${e%%$'\t'*}" )
  done
  for e in "${(@o)byname}"; do
    n="${e%%$'\t'*}"; s="${e#*$'\t'}"
    (( bestscore - s <= band )) || continue
    cand_t="${_CLAUDE_LEDGER[$n]:-0}"
    if [[ -z "$pick" ]] || (( cand_t < pickt )); then
      pick="$n"; pickt="$cand_t"
    fi
  done
  REPLY="$pick"
  [[ -n "$pick" ]]
}

# --- machine backpressure -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Sets _CML_LOAD1 (x100, integer), _CML_NCPU, _CML_SWAPPCT. Any of them may be
# the literal `unknown`; none is ever guessed.
#
# WARN-ONLY BY DEFAULT, and that is a decision rather than an oversight (D6 as
# amended): this laptop is deliberately oversubscribed and must keep working, so
# the picker's job is to choose an account, not to police the box. A refusal
# happens only when a *_MAX knob is explicitly set. The measurement still ships
# so claude-doctor, --explain and the herdr-draft row can show it.
#
# Forkless, and read under CLAUDE_PICK_PROC_ROOT so the rows can fixture it.
_claude_machine_load() {
  local root="${CLAUDE_PICK_PROC_ROOT:-}" line tot free
  _CML_LOAD1=unknown; _CML_NCPU=unknown; _CML_SWAPPCT=unknown

  if [[ -r "$root/proc/loadavg" ]]; then
    line="$(<"$root/proc/loadavg")"
    line="${line%% *}"
    # x100 as an integer: no zsh/mathfunc anywhere in this file.
    if [[ "$line" == <->.<-> ]]; then
      _CML_LOAD1=$(( ${line%%.*} * 100 + 10#${${line#*.}[1,2]} ))
    elif [[ "$line" == <-> ]]; then
      _CML_LOAD1=$(( line * 100 ))
    fi
  fi

  # "0-7" or "0-3,8-11". Counting the ONLINE cpus, not the highest index: a
  # machine with cpus offline has fewer, and dividing load by the wrong count
  # would warn either far too early or never.
  if [[ -r "$root/sys/devices/system/cpu/online" ]]; then
    local spec part n=0 lo hi
    spec="$(<"$root/sys/devices/system/cpu/online")"
    for part in ${(s:,:)spec}; do
      if [[ "$part" == <->-<-> ]]; then
        lo="${part%%-*}"; hi="${part##*-}"
        (( n += hi - lo + 1 ))
      elif [[ "$part" == <-> ]]; then
        (( n++ ))
      fi
    done
    (( n > 0 )) && _CML_NCPU=$n
  fi

  if [[ -r "$root/proc/meminfo" ]]; then
    tot=""; free=""
    while IFS= read -r line; do
      case "$line" in
        SwapTotal:*) tot="${${(z)line}[2]}" ;;
        SwapFree:*)  free="${${(z)line}[2]}" ;;
      esac
    done < "$root/proc/meminfo"
    # SwapTotal 0 is a machine with no swap, not 100% swap used. Dividing by it
    # would be a division by zero; reporting 100 would warn forever.
    if [[ "$tot" == <-> && "$free" == <-> ]] && (( tot > 0 )); then
      _CML_SWAPPCT=$(( (tot - free) * 100 / tot ))
    fi
  fi
  return 0
}

# Append machine warnings to _claude_pick_warnings, and report whether a MAX knob
# was exceeded. Returns 0 normally, 3 when a refusal is warranted.
#
# It PUBLISHES _CML_LOAD1/_CML_NCPU/_CML_SWAPPCT rather than localising them, so
# `--explain` and the JSON can report the numbers a refusal was decided on
# without reading /proc a second time. One writer — _claude_machine_load — so
# there is still exactly one answer to "what is this machine doing"; a second
# read could legitimately differ from the one that decided, and then the report
# would not be about the decision. A state-table row asserts they survive.
_claude_pick_backpressure() {
  local load_warn=${CLAUDE_PICK_LOAD_WARN:-150} swap_warn=${CLAUDE_PICK_SWAP_WARN:-60}
  local load_max=${CLAUDE_PICK_LOAD_MAX:-0} swap_max=${CLAUDE_PICK_SWAP_MAX:-0}
  local pct=unknown rc=0
  _claude_machine_load

  if [[ "$_CML_LOAD1" != unknown && "$_CML_NCPU" != unknown ]] && (( _CML_NCPU > 0 )); then
    pct=$(( _CML_LOAD1 / _CML_NCPU ))     # load1 x100 / ncpu == percent of ncpu
  fi
  if [[ "$pct" != unknown ]] && (( pct >= load_warn )); then
    # THE LOAD ITSELF, then the ratio in brackets. This printed only the ratio
    # ("load 3.4 x 8 threads") until it was run on the real machine beside
    # `claude-pick --explain`, which reports "load 27.88 on 8 threads" — the same
    # measurement in two shapes, one of them a number that appears nowhere else,
    # so a reader comparing the two lines has to work out that 3.4 is 27.88/8.
    # A state-table row pins both numbers now; before this, every row counted
    # warnings and none read one.
    _claude_pick_warnings+=("machine: load $(( _CML_LOAD1 / 100 )).${(l:2::0:)$(( _CML_LOAD1 % 100 ))} on ${_CML_NCPU} threads ($(( pct / 100 )).$(( (pct % 100) / 10 ))x) — a new session will thrash")
    (( load_max > 0 && pct >= load_max )) && rc=3
  fi
  if [[ "$_CML_SWAPPCT" != unknown ]] && (( _CML_SWAPPCT >= swap_warn )); then
    _claude_pick_warnings+=("machine: swap ${_CML_SWAPPCT}% used — a new session will thrash")
    (( swap_max > 0 && _CML_SWAPPCT >= swap_max )) && rc=3
  fi
  return $rc
}

# --- exhaustion reporting -----------------------------------------------------
#
# When every member is exhausted, name the one whose blocking window resets
# soonest — and NAME NO TIME when none is known.
#
# `resets_at` is absent whenever the 5h window has not started (measured: three
# of five profiles here), so "no reset instant" is the COMMON case, not an edge.
# A message that printed a time unconditionally would print an epoch-derived
# nonsense date, or the same date for every account, in exactly the situation
# where the reader is trying to decide how long to wait.
#
# Sets REPLY to the least-bad profile. Prints nothing; the caller reports.
_claude_pick_least_bad() {
  local e n best="" bestr="" r
  local _CPM_U5 _CPM_R5 _CPM_UW _CPM_AGE _CPM_TIER
  for n in "$@"; do
    _claude_profile_metrics "$n" >/dev/null 2>&1
    r="$_CPM_R5"
    if [[ "$r" == unknown ]]; then
      # Unknown reset sorts AFTER any known one: an account that might reset in
      # four minutes is a better bet than one nobody can time.
      [[ -z "$best" ]] && { best="$n"; bestr=unknown; }
      continue
    fi
    if [[ -z "$best" || "$bestr" == unknown ]] || (( r < bestr )); then
      best="$n"; bestr="$r"
    fi
  done
  REPLY="$best"
  _CLAUDE_PICK_LEASTBAD_R5="$bestr"
  [[ -n "$best" ]]
}

# "Thu 11 Sep 04:00 IDT", or the empty string when the instant is not known.
# Every caller must handle the empty string — that is the point of the function.
_claude_pick_reset_text() {
  local r="$1"
  [[ "$r" == unknown || -z "$r" ]] && { print -r -- ""; return 1 }
  zmodload zsh/datetime 2>/dev/null || { print -r -- ""; return 1 }
  print -r -- "$(strftime '%a %d %b %H:%M %Z' $(( EPOCHSECONDS + r )) 2>/dev/null)"
}

# clauth's own active profile, when it has a credential and is not excluded.
# This is the last-resort ACCOUNT: it keeps a session isolated when every pool
# member is unusable, instead of dropping it onto the shared global file.
_claude_fallback_profile() {
  local ap
  [[ -r "$HOME/.clauth/status.json" ]] || return 1
  command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  ap="$(jq -e -r '.active_profile | select(type == "string") | select(. != "")' \
        "$HOME/.clauth/status.json" 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  [[ -f "$HOME/.clauth/profiles/$ap/credentials.json" ]] || return 1
  _claude_profile_excluded "$ap" >/dev/null && return 1
  print -r -- "$ap"
}

# --- the one code path --------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHICH POOL does a tenant name, and what may it borrow?
#
# Split out so the picker asks the question in exactly one place. The two
# refusals below are most of the reason this layer exists, and a second copy of
# them is a second place for one of them to be forgotten.
#
# Sets  _claude_pick_pool_members, _claude_pick_overflow_members.
# Returns 0 usable · 4 the table, or the caller's tenant, is unusable.
_claude_pick_pool() {
  local tenant="${1:-}"
  _claude_pick_pool_members=(); _claude_pick_overflow_members=()
  if [[ -n "$tenant" ]]; then
    # A tenant with no pool entry yields an EMPTY member list, and an empty list
    # is indistinguishable from "no pool configured" — which the candidate loop
    # reads as EVERY profile. So the one thing a caller must never get here is a
    # silent widening: `--tenant typo` would quietly become "any account on the
    # machine", including the ones this whole layer exists to keep separate.
    # The table check catches a tenant NAMED in the table; this catches one
    # supplied by a caller (CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_TENANT, hspawn --tenant,
    # claude-pick --tenant), which never passed through it.
    if (( ! ${+CLAUDE_TENANT_POOL[$tenant]} )); then
      _claude_pick_skipped+=("tenant '$tenant' has no CLAUDE_TENANT_POOL entry — refusing to widen the pool to every account")
      return 4
    fi
    _claude_pick_pool_members=( ${=CLAUDE_TENANT_POOL[$tenant]} )
    _claude_pick_overflow_members=( ${=CLAUDE_TENANT_OVERFLOW[$tenant]-} )
    return 0
  fi
  # No tenant. Two very different situations reach here, and only one of them is
  # safe to answer with CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_POOL:
  #
  #   1. No tenant table is configured at all — the modular adopter. The flat
  #      pool is the whole mechanism, an empty one means "every registered
  #      profile", and that is correct: they have one account. UNCHANGED.
  #   2. A tenant table IS configured but the resolver could not answer —
  #      `bad-table`, `git-error`, or an empty CLAUDE_TENANT_DEFAULT. The caller
  #      leaves the tenant empty and lands here, and if CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_POOL is
  #      also empty the candidate loop reads that as EVERY profile. So a single
  #      typo in the tenant file silently bills personal work to a work account —
  #      the exact failure this layer exists to remove, through the back door.
  #
  # Measured 2026-09-09, with a one-character error in the tenant file and no
  # flat pool set: a personal tree picked a work account. It read as an ordinary
  # successful launch, because the reason lives in _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY and the
  # announcement never showed it.
  if (( ${#CLAUDE_TENANT_POOL} )) && (( ! ${#CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_POOL} )); then
    _claude_pick_skipped+=("the tenant table is configured but resolved nothing (${_CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE:-no state}${_CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY:+: $_CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY}) — refusing to widen the pool to every account")
    return 4
  fi
  _claude_pick_pool_members=( "${CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_POOL[@]}" )
  return 0
}

# THE PICKER. resolve -> candidates -> classify -> score -> exhaustion ->
# backpressure -> ledger, for one directory.
#
# Usage: _claude_pick_for_dir [dir] [tenant] [strict] [force]
#   dir     resolved for a tenant when no tenant is pinned. Empty means "do not
#           resolve" — which is what the legacy tenant-less entry point passes.
#   tenant  a PIN: it skips the resolver, NOT the ranking inside that pool.
#           Pinning says where to bill, not which account to burn.
#   strict  1 for a headless caller (hspawn, claude-pick --strict, herdr-draft):
#           an exhausted pool is a refusal rather than a least-bad pick.
#   force   1 to proceed past a machine ceiling that would otherwise refuse.
#
# Sets, all of them shell-wide names the caller may declare `local`:
#   REPLY                   the chosen profile, when it returns 0
#   _claude_pick_state      picked | fallback | exhausted | backpressure
#                           | bad-table | no-profiles | unusable
#   _claude_pick_class      eligible | unknown | least-bad | fallback
#   _claude_pick_reason     one line, safe to print
#   _claude_pick_tenant     the tenant that decided, or empty
#   _claude_pick_tenant_state
#   _claude_pick_score      the winning score, or empty
#   _claude_pick_u5 _claude_pick_uw _claude_pick_r5 _claude_pick_rw
#   _claude_pick_age _claude_pick_tier _claude_pick_holders
#   _claude_pick_load       kept for claude()'s existing one-line announcement
#   _claude_pick_overflow   1 when the pick came from another tenant's overflow
#   _claude_pick_leastbad _claude_pick_leastbad_r5
#   _claude_pick_skipped[]  human-readable, one per refused thing
#   _claude_pick_warnings[] machine backpressure and lock notes
#   _claude_pick_explain[]  "<profile>\t<class>\t<why>\t<u5>\t<uW>\t<age>\t<holders>\t<score>\t<r5>"
#   _claude_pick_exhausted[] "<profile>\t<u5>\t<uW>\t<r5>"  (for the §5.4 report)
#   _CML_LOAD1 _CML_NCPU _CML_SWAPPCT   published by the backpressure read
#
# Returns 0 picked · 2 refused (exhausted, or nothing usable) · 3 refused
#         (machine ceiling) · 4 unusable tenant table · 5 no profiles at all.
#
# WHY ONE FUNCTION. claude(), hspawn and claude-pick would otherwise each answer
# parts of this for themselves, and the parts drift: the tenant a caller
# resolves, the pool it consults and the reason it reports were three separate
# pieces of code. Which account a session bills is exactly the thing nothing
# announces afterwards, so the answer has to come from one place that also
# produces the explanation.
#
# It reads the metrics for a candidate ONCE and calls _claude_pick_class, which
# reads them again — two jq forks per pool member per launch, deliberately. The
# alternative is a second copy of the classification rules here, and this repo
# has paid for that shape more than once. _claude_pick_class stays the single
# pinned oracle for "may this account be chosen".
_claude_pick_for_dir() {
  local dir="${1:-}" tenant="${2:-}" strict="${3:-0}" force="${4:-0}"
  local p name cls why score holders pass_name fb bp_rc=0
  local _CPM_U5 _CPM_R5 _CPM_UW _CPM_RW _CPM_AGE _CPM_TIER
  local -a registered pass_members exnames
  zmodload -F zsh/stat b:zstat 2>/dev/null
  zmodload zsh/datetime 2>/dev/null

  REPLY=""
  _claude_pick_state=""; _claude_pick_class=""; _claude_pick_reason=""
  _claude_pick_score=""; _claude_pick_u5=unknown; _claude_pick_uw=unknown
  _claude_pick_r5=unknown; _claude_pick_rw=unknown; _claude_pick_age=unknown
  _claude_pick_tier=unknown; _claude_pick_holders=0
  _claude_pick_load=""; _claude_pick_overflow=0
  _claude_pick_tenant=""; _claude_pick_tenant_state=""
  _claude_pick_leastbad=""; _claude_pick_leastbad_r5=unknown
  # Reset per call, not appended to: a second pick in the same shell — the
  # debounced --dry-run herdr-draft makes for a form row, say — must not report
  # the first call's refusals as its own.
  _claude_pick_skipped=(); _claude_pick_warnings=()
  _claude_pick_explain=(); _claude_pick_exhausted=()
  _claude_pick_cands=(); _claude_pick_unknowns=()

  # WHICH TENANT. A pinned one skips the resolver; an empty `dir` says do not
  # resolve at all, which is how the legacy entry point keeps its meaning. The
  # _CLAUDE_TENANT_* globals are deliberately NOT localised here: a caller that
  # resolved for itself and then called in has them set, and the pool refusal
  # above quotes them.
  if [[ -z "$tenant" && -n "$dir" ]] && (( $+functions[_claude_tenant_for] )); then
    _claude_tenant_for "$dir" && tenant="$_CLAUDE_TENANT"
    _claude_pick_tenant_state="${_CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE:-}"
  fi
  _claude_pick_tenant="$tenant"

  # MACHINE BACKPRESSURE IS MEASURED FIRST, and acted on last. The measurement is
  # independent of the tenant table and of the profile census, and every exit path
  # reports it — a refusal that cannot say what the machine was doing is the empty
  # answer this repo keeps paying for, and herdr-draft's failure row shows these
  # numbers. An earlier version read it after the census and its own comment
  # claimed "every exit path", which was false for exits 4 and 5.
  #
  # The VERDICT is deferred to below, because the precedence matters: an unusable
  # table (4) and no credential at all (5) are faults a person must fix, while a
  # machine ceiling (3) is transient — reporting the fixable one first is what a
  # caller can act on. Warn-only unless a MAX knob is set, and only a headless
  # caller refuses on it (D6 as amended: this box is deliberately oversubscribed
  # and must keep working).
  _claude_pick_backpressure; bp_rc=$?

  _claude_pick_pool "$tenant" || {
    _claude_pick_state=bad-table
    _claude_pick_reason="${_claude_pick_skipped[-1]}"
    return 4
  }

  # Every profile clauth has registered WITH a credential. `-e || -L` matches
  # what _claude_pick_class tests, so the two cannot disagree about what counts
  # as registered — a dangling credential symlink is a state the reconciler
  # owns, and it lands in the `unknown` class rather than vanishing from the
  # census.
  for p in "$HOME"/.clauth/profiles/*(N/); do
    [[ -e "$p/credentials.json" || -L "$p/credentials.json" ]] || continue
    registered+=( "${p:t}" )
  done
  if (( ! ${#registered} )); then
    _claude_pick_state=no-profiles
    _claude_pick_reason="no clauth profile has a credential — 'clauth login <name>'"
    return 5
  fi

  # Two passes at most. Overflow is consulted ONLY when the pool named nothing
  # that exists at all — borrowing another tenant's account bills work to the
  # wrong place, so it is paid only when the alternative is no isolation. An
  # EXHAUSTED pool is deliberately not that case: it is a real wall that clears
  # itself, with a reset time to quote, and borrowing a seat to get past it would
  # bill the wrong tenant permanently for a wait of minutes.
  for pass_name in pool overflow; do
    if [[ "$pass_name" == overflow ]]; then
      (( ${#_claude_pick_cands} + ${#_claude_pick_unknowns} + ${#_claude_pick_exhausted} )) && break
      (( ${#_claude_pick_overflow_members} )) || break
      pass_members=( "${_claude_pick_overflow_members[@]}" )
      _claude_pick_overflow=1
    else
      pass_members=( "${_claude_pick_pool_members[@]}" )
    fi
    for name in "${registered[@]}"; do
      if (( ${#pass_members} )); then
        (( ${pass_members[(Ie)$name]} )) || continue
      fi
      cls="$(_claude_pick_class "$name")"
      if [[ "$cls" == *:* ]]; then why="${cls#*:}"; cls="${cls%%:*}"; else why=""; fi
      _claude_profile_metrics "$name" >/dev/null 2>&1
      holders="$(_claude_holder_count "$name")"
      score=""
      case "$cls" in
        eligible)
          score="$(_claude_pick_score "$_CPM_U5" "$_CPM_R5" "$_CPM_UW" "$holders")"
          _claude_pick_cands+=( "$score"$'\t'"$name" )
          ;;
        unknown)
          # A TIER, not an arithmetic accident. "Unknown never outranks a
          # measurement" is a rule about rank, and expressing it as a score would
          # put it at the mercy of the weights: an eligible account at 96% with a
          # spent week scores near zero too, and then a profile nobody can read
          # would beat one that was measured and nearly empty.
          _claude_pick_unknowns+=( "0"$'\t'"$name" )
          ;;
        exhausted)
          _claude_pick_exhausted+=( "$name"$'\t'"$_CPM_U5"$'\t'"$_CPM_UW"$'\t'"$_CPM_R5" )
          ;;
        *)
          _claude_pick_skipped+=("$name ($why)")
          ;;
      esac
      _claude_pick_explain+=( "$name"$'\t'"$cls"$'\t'"$why"$'\t'"$_CPM_U5"$'\t'"$_CPM_UW"$'\t'"$_CPM_AGE"$'\t'"$holders"$'\t'"$score"$'\t'"$_CPM_R5" )
    done
  done
  # A pass that found nothing must not leave the overflow flag set: the
  # announcement would otherwise tell the reader a session is on a borrowed
  # account when it is actually on the shared credential.
  (( ${#_claude_pick_cands} + ${#_claude_pick_unknowns} + ${#_claude_pick_exhausted} )) \
    || _claude_pick_overflow=0

  # The deferred machine verdict, from the measurement taken at the top.
  if (( bp_rc == 3 && strict && ! force )); then
    _claude_pick_state=backpressure
    _claude_pick_reason="${_claude_pick_warnings[-1]:-a machine ceiling was exceeded}"
    return 3
  fi
  (( bp_rc == 3 && force )) && \
    _claude_pick_warnings+=("--force: proceeding past a machine ceiling that would otherwise refuse")

  # ELIGIBLE first, then UNKNOWN, then the exhaustion path. The ledger decides
  # inside each tier, under one lock around read -> decide -> write: a
  # deterministic sort cannot close the two-shells-in-one-second race, because
  # both would read the same "least recent" and both would choose it.
  if (( ${#_claude_pick_cands} )); then
    _claude_pick_class=eligible
  elif (( ${#_claude_pick_unknowns} )); then
    _claude_pick_cands=( "${_claude_pick_unknowns[@]}" )
    _claude_pick_class=unknown
  fi
  if [[ -n "$_claude_pick_class" ]] && _claude_pick_with_ledger_lock _claude_pick_decide; then
    _claude_pick_state=picked
    _claude_pick_publish "$REPLY"
    return 0
  fi

  # EVERY MEMBER EXHAUSTED. A headless caller refuses; an interactive one
  # proceeds on the least-bad member and says so loudly, because the alternative
  # is a human blocked by a window that clears itself.
  if (( ${#_claude_pick_exhausted} )); then
    for p in "${_claude_pick_exhausted[@]}"; do exnames+=( "${p%%$'\t'*}" ); done
    _claude_pick_least_bad "${exnames[@]}"
    _claude_pick_leastbad="$REPLY"
    _claude_pick_leastbad_r5="$_CLAUDE_PICK_LEASTBAD_R5"
    if (( strict )); then
      REPLY=""
      _claude_pick_state=exhausted
      _claude_pick_reason="every member of ${_claude_pick_tenant:+pool $_claude_pick_tenant }${_claude_pick_tenant:+has}${_claude_pick_tenant:-the account pool has} a spent 5h window"
      _claude_pick_report_exhausted strict
      return 2
    fi
    _claude_pick_class=least-bad
    _claude_pick_state=picked
    _claude_pick_reason="every pool member's 5h window is spent — proceeding on the least-bad one"
    _claude_pick_report_exhausted interactive
    if [[ -n "$REPLY" ]]; then
      (( ${_claude_pick_dry_run:-0} )) || _claude_pick_with_ledger_lock _claude_pick_ledger_write "$REPLY"
      _claude_pick_publish "$REPLY"
      return 0
    fi
  fi

  # NOTHING IN THE POOL IS USABLE. Fall back to an ACCOUNT, not to the shared
  # file: the exclusions that empty a pool (quarantine, disabled) correlate with
  # load, so this path is rare in calm weather and concentrated in bad weather —
  # the opposite of a safe degradation. Dropping onto the shared global
  # credential there would put the session in the one group where a single bad
  # write logs everybody out, at the moment most sessions are starting,
  # controlled only by a stderr line nobody reads.
  if fb="$(_claude_fallback_profile)"; then
    REPLY="$fb"
    _claude_pick_class=fallback
    _claude_pick_state=fallback
    _claude_pick_skipped+=("fell back to the active account '$fb'")
    _claude_pick_load=1001          # sorts as "unknown"; announced as such
    _claude_pick_publish "$fb"
    _claude_pick_reason="no pool member was usable — fell back to the active account '$fb'"
    return 0
  fi
  REPLY=""
  _claude_pick_state=unusable
  _claude_pick_reason="no usable account${_claude_pick_skipped:+: ${(j:, :)_claude_pick_skipped}}"
  return 2
}

# Choose and record, run under the ledger lock as ONE unit: read -> decide ->
# write. Split out only because _claude_pick_with_ledger_lock takes a command,
# and it must run in this shell rather than a subshell or REPLY dies with it.
_claude_pick_decide() {
  _claude_pick_choose || return 1
  (( ${_claude_pick_dry_run:-0} )) || _claude_pick_ledger_write "$REPLY"
  return 0
}

# The winning profile's own numbers, for the announcement, --explain and the JSON.
# Read out of _claude_pick_explain rather than measured again: a second read can
# legitimately differ from the one the decision was made on, and then the report
# is not about the decision.
_claude_pick_publish() {
  local e
  local -a f
  for e in "${_claude_pick_explain[@]}"; do
    [[ "${e%%$'\t'*}" == "$1" ]] || continue
    f=( "${(@ps:\t:)e}" )
    _claude_pick_u5="${f[4]}"; _claude_pick_uw="${f[5]}"
    _claude_pick_age="${f[6]}"; _claude_pick_holders="${f[7]}"
    _claude_pick_score="${f[8]}"; _claude_pick_r5="${f[9]}"
    break
  done
  # The tier and the weekly reset are informational and not in the explain row,
  # which is a ranking record. `local` here so the caller's copies are untouched.
  local _CPM_U5 _CPM_R5 _CPM_UW _CPM_RW _CPM_AGE _CPM_TIER
  _claude_profile_metrics "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1
  _claude_pick_tier="$_CPM_TIER"; _claude_pick_rw="$_CPM_RW"
  # claude()'s one-line announcement reads _claude_pick_load: <=100 is a measured
  # 5h figure, 101 is "no reading", 1001 is the fallback. Unchanged, so the
  # announcement is not a second thing to keep in step. Already set on the
  # fallback path, which is why this does not overwrite it.
  if [[ -z "$_claude_pick_load" ]]; then
    if [[ "$_claude_pick_u5" == unknown ]]; then _claude_pick_load=101
    else _claude_pick_load="$_claude_pick_u5"; fi
  fi
}

# The §5.4 messages, in one place because both of them are the SAME facts read
# two ways — one refuses, one proceeds — and a second copy would be a second
# place to forget that no time may be invented.
#
# `resets_at` is absent whenever the 5h window has not started (measured: three
# of five profiles here), so "no reset instant" is the COMMON case, not an edge.
# A message that printed a time unconditionally would print the same
# epoch-derived nonsense for every account, in exactly the situation where the
# reader is trying to decide how long to wait.
#
# stderr, always, and never suppressed by CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_QUIET: quiet exists to
# silence the routine one-line announcement, and this is the opposite of routine.
# The parenthetical for one member's 5h window. Three states, and the third is
# not an edge: a NON-POSITIVE r5 means the window named in the cache has already
# rolled, so the ~100% figure beside it belongs to the PREVIOUS window and is
# stale. Printing "resets <a date in the past>" there — which is what formatting
# the delta unconditionally does — tells the reader to wait for a moment that has
# been and gone. On this machine that state is common rather than rare, because
# nothing refreshes the usage caches on a schedule (clauth's only writer is
# gated on a lease taken by its TUI and its daemon, and the daemon is off).
_claude_pick_window_text() {
  local r="${1:-unknown}" txt
  if [[ "$r" == (-|)<-> ]] && (( r <= 0 )); then
    print -r -- "5h window already rolled — this reading is stale"
    return 0
  fi
  txt="$(_claude_pick_reset_text "$r")"
  if [[ -n "$txt" ]]; then print -r -- "resets $txt"; else print -r -- "reset time unknown"; fi
}

_claude_pick_report_exhausted() {
  local mode="${1:-strict}" prog="${_claude_pick_prog:-claude}"
  local e n u5 uw uwt r5 label soon lbr="$_claude_pick_leastbad_r5"
  local -a f
  label="the account pool"
  [[ -n "$_claude_pick_tenant" ]] && label="pool '$_claude_pick_tenant'"
  soon="$(_claude_pick_reset_text "$lbr")"
  # A reset already in the past is not a time to quote back. Same reasoning as
  # _claude_pick_window_text, at the header.
  [[ "$lbr" == (-|)<-> ]] && (( lbr <= 0 )) && soon=""

  if [[ "$mode" == interactive ]]; then
    print -u2 -- "$prog: $label has NO usable account — proceeding on the least-bad one:"
  elif [[ -n "$soon" ]]; then
    print -u2 -- "$prog: refused — $label exhausted (earliest reset $soon, on $_claude_pick_leastbad)"
  elif [[ "$lbr" == (-|)<-> ]]; then
    print -u2 -- "$prog: refused — $label exhausted on stale readings (its soonest 5h window has already rolled)"
  else
    print -u2 -- "$prog: refused — $label exhausted (no reset time is known for any member)"
  fi

  for e in "${_claude_pick_exhausted[@]}"; do
    f=( "${(@ps:\t:)e}" )
    n="${f[1]}"; u5="${f[2]}"; uw="${f[3]}"; r5="${f[4]}"
    # Only the least-bad member is listed on the interactive path: it is the one
    # being proceeded on, and a human about to be told "this is going to be
    # tight" does not need the other rows in order to act.
    if [[ "$mode" == interactive && "$n" != "$_claude_pick_leastbad" ]]; then
      continue
    fi
    # `7d unknown`, never `7d unknown%`: a percent sign on a word that is not a
    # number reads as a measurement of nothing.
    if [[ "$uw" == unknown ]]; then uwt="7d unknown"; else uwt="7d ${uw}%"; fi
    print -u2 -- "        $n  5h ${u5}% · $uwt ($(_claude_pick_window_text "$r5"))"
  done

  if [[ "$mode" == interactive ]]; then
    print -u2 -- "        Headless callers refuse here. Override: claude-as <profile> · CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_TENANT=<t> claude"
  elif [[ -n "$soon" ]]; then
    print -u2 -- "        --profile <p> to override, or retry after $soon."
  else
    print -u2 -- "        --profile <p> to override."
  fi
}

# The tenant-less entry point, kept for one release so nothing outside this file
# has to change in the same commit. It resolves NO directory — that is what the
# empty first argument means — so a caller that resolved a tenant for itself and
# passes it in gets exactly the pool it named, and a caller that passes nothing
# gets the flat-pool semantics unchanged.
_claude_pick_profile() {
  _claude_pick_for_dir "" "${1:-}" 0 0 && return 0
  return 1
}

claude() {
  # Reset per call: these carry the picker's explanation out to the announcement,
  # and a second launch in the same shell must not inherit the first's reasons.
  local _claude_pick_load="" REPLY="" _claude_pick_overflow=0
  local -a _claude_pick_skipped=() _claude_pick_warnings=()
  local -a _claude_pick_explain=() _claude_pick_exhausted=()
  local -a _claude_pick_cands=() _claude_pick_unknowns=()
  local -a _claude_pick_pool_members=() _claude_pick_overflow_members=()
  local _claude_pick_state="" _claude_pick_class="" _claude_pick_reason=""
  local _claude_pick_score="" _claude_pick_tenant="" _claude_pick_tenant_state=""
  local _claude_pick_u5=unknown _claude_pick_uw=unknown _claude_pick_r5=unknown
  local _claude_pick_rw=unknown _claude_pick_age=unknown _claude_pick_tier=unknown
  local _claude_pick_holders=0 _claude_pick_leastbad="" _claude_pick_leastbad_r5=unknown
  local _CML_LOAD1=unknown _CML_NCPU=unknown _CML_SWAPPCT=unknown
  # Whose name the picker's own messages carry.
  local _claude_pick_prog=claude
  # The resolver reports through these too, and they are shell-wide names.
  local _CLAUDE_TENANT="" _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE="" _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY=""

  # local -x, never a bare export: an export here would leak the config dir into
  # the pane's shell and silently pin every LATER command in it to this account.
  # -x still exports to this function's children, which is all that is needed.
  local -x CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-}"
  local profile="" dir="" holder="" tenant=""

  # An inherited CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR is never overridden: that is what keeps a
  # `clauth start` pane, a herdmates teammate, and hspawn's own launch intact.
  if [[ -z "$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR" && -z "${CLAUDE_ISOLATION_OFF:-}" ]] \
     && command -v clauth >/dev/null 2>&1 && [[ -x "$(_claude_account_builder)" ]]; then
    if [[ -n "${CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_PROFILE:-}" ]]; then
      profile="$CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_PROFILE"
    else
      # Precedence, highest first: an inherited CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR (above) >
      # CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_PROFILE / claude-as (above) > a pinned tenant > the
      # resolver. A pinned tenant skips the resolver but NOT the ranking inside
      # its pool — pinning says where to bill, not which account to burn.
      #
      # $PWD, and the picker resolves it. INTERACTIVE (strict=0): an exhausted
      # pool proceeds on the least-bad member with the loud §5.4 block, because
      # a human blocked by a window that clears itself in minutes is a worse
      # outcome than a tight session. hspawn and claude-pick --strict refuse in
      # the same state; that asymmetry is the decision, not an oversight.
      if _claude_pick_for_dir "$PWD" "${CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_TENANT:-}" 0 0; then
        profile="$REPLY"
      else
        profile=""
      fi
      tenant="$_claude_pick_tenant"
      # Machine backpressure is warn-only for an interactive launch, but it must
      # still be SAID: this box is deliberately oversubscribed, and a session
      # that is about to thrash should know before it starts, not after.
      local _w
      for _w in "${_claude_pick_warnings[@]}"; do print -u2 -- "claude: $_w"; done
    fi
    if [[ -z "$profile" ]]; then
      # NEVER SILENT in either direction: which credential a session burns decides
      # which account is billed, and nothing about the running pane reveals it
      # afterwards. Falling back to the shared file is right — inventing a profile
      # would bill an account nobody picked — but it must be said.
      if (( ${#_claude_pick_skipped} )); then
        print -u2 -- "claude: NO usable account — not the pool, and not clauth's active one."
        print -u2 -- "        This session shares ~/.claude/.credentials.json with every other,"
        print -u2 -- "        which is the group where one bad write logs all of them out."
        print -u2 -- "        refused: ${(j:, :)_claude_pick_skipped}"
        print -u2 -- "        Fix one account ('clauth login <name>'), or choose deliberately:"
        print -u2 -- "          claude-as <profile>      # isolated onto a named account"
        print -u2 -- "          CLAUDE_ISOLATION_OFF=1   # the shared file, on purpose"
      else
        print -u2 -- "claude: clauth is installed but no profile has a credential — this session shares"
        print -u2 -- "        ~/.claude/.credentials.json. 'clauth login <name>' to give it its own."
      fi
    elif dir="$(command "$(_claude_account_builder)" "$profile")" && [[ -n "$dir" ]]; then
      CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$dir"
    else
      profile=""
      print -u2 -- "claude: could not build an account dir (its reason is above) — this session"
      print -u2 -- "        shares ~/.claude/.credentials.json with every other."
    fi
  fi

  # Count as a holder however CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR came to point at an account dir.
  # hspawn sets it itself, and a spawn that did not register would make the
  # chooser above hand the next session the busiest profile.
  if [[ "$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR" == "$(_claude_account_root)/"* ]]; then
    profile="${${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR#$(_claude_account_root)/}%%/*}"
    holder="$(_claude_account_root)/$profile/holders/$$"
    command mkdir -p "${holder:h}" 2>/dev/null && : > "$holder" 2>/dev/null || holder=""
    if [[ -z "${CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_QUIET:-}" ]]; then
      local detail=""
      # The load that WON, and anything the pool refused — a session that lands on
      # an unexpected account should say why without being asked.
      if [[ -n "${_claude_pick_load:-}" ]]; then
        if (( _claude_pick_load > 1000 )); then detail=" — fallback: the pool was unusable"
        elif (( _claude_pick_load > 100 )); then detail=" — no usage reading"
        else detail=" — 5h $(( 10#$_claude_pick_load ))% used"; fi
      fi
      # The class, when it is not the ordinary one. `least-bad` means every
      # member was exhausted and this session is proceeding anyway — the loud
      # block above already said so, and this keeps it on the line a reader
      # scrolls back to.
      [[ -n "$_claude_pick_class" && "$_claude_pick_class" != eligible ]] && \
        detail="$detail · $_claude_pick_class"
      # Which tenant decided, and whether the account was BORROWED. A session on
      # another tenant's account is billing work to the wrong place; that is
      # sometimes the right trade, but it is never something to discover later.
      [[ -n "$tenant" ]] && detail="$detail · tenant '$tenant'"
      (( _claude_pick_overflow )) && detail="$detail (overflow)"
      print -u2 -- "claude: account '$profile'$detail (isolated: ${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/#$HOME/~})"
      (( ${#_claude_pick_skipped} )) && \
        print -u2 -- "        skipped: ${(j:, :)_claude_pick_skipped}"
    fi
  fi

  # Pin gh to THIS directory before Claude freezes its environment. gh's token
  # and the GitHub MCP plugin's are both fixed for the life of the process, so
  # this is the one moment the pin can still be made right — the DO-596 incident
  # shape (a session started in a work workspace root carried the personal token
  # for its whole life and 404'd every private work repo), closed here.
  # Guarded: _update_gh_config lives in zshrc.company, which a modular adopter
  # does not have.
  (( $+functions[_update_gh_config] )) && _update_gh_config

  {
    if [[ -n "$HERDR_PANE_ID" ]] && command -v herdmates >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS=1 herdmates teammux-launch "$@"
    else
      command claude "$@"
    fi
  } always {
    [[ -n "$holder" ]] && command rm -f -- "$holder"
  }
}

# Run claude under a NAMED account, isolated, still able to lead a team.
#
# The verb that replaces `clauth <profile>` for picking an account to work under:
# that one switches the global credential for the whole machine and can log out
# every session holding the profile it restores, while this one changes nothing
# outside this session.
claude-as() {
  if (( $# == 0 )) || [[ "$1" == -h || "$1" == --help ]]; then
    print -u2 -- "Usage: claude-as <clauth-profile> [claude args...]"
    print -u2 -- "  Isolated onto that profile's credential, and still a herdr team lead."
    print -u2 -- "  Accounts: ${$(print -l "$HOME"/.clauth/profiles/*(N/:t)):-<none registered>}"
    return 2
  fi
  local profile="$1"; shift
  CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_PROFILE="$profile" claude "$@"
}

#==============================================================================
# herdr-lazy — plugin CLI is not on PATH by design (README §Use)
#==============================================================================
# herdr-lazy manages the declared plugin set (~/.config/herdr/plugins/config/
# herdr-lazy/plugins.list + plugins.lock, committed under ~/.dotfiles), but its
# binary lives inside the plugin's own managed dir, not on PATH. Resolve it via
# `herdr plugin list` each call rather than hardcoding the path (it changes if
# herdr-lazy's own pin is bumped).
#
# The env -u guard works around a herdmates bug (verified, not yet fixed
# upstream): herdmates sets HERDR_PLUGIN_STATE_DIR/HERDR_PLUGIN_CONFIG_DIR
# process-wide before exec'ing claude, so every lead session and everything it
# execs inherits herdmates' own plugin dirs instead of the caller's. Without
# the guard, herdr-lazy silently reads/writes herdmates' state instead of its
# own. Same guard applies to any other plugin CLI (e.g. `hap`) run from a lead
# pane.
herdr-lazy() {
  local root
  root=$(herdr plugin list --json 2>/dev/null | python3 -c \
    "import json,sys;print([p['plugin_root'] for p in json.load(sys.stdin)['result']['plugins'] if p['plugin_id']=='herdr-lazy'][0])")
  env -u HERDR_PLUGIN_STATE_DIR -u HERDR_PLUGIN_CONFIG_DIR "$root/target/release/herdr-lazy" "$@"
}

#==============================================================================
# hspawn / hdespawn / hreap — Atrium's `atrium new` loop, rebuilt on herdr
#==============================================================================
# Atrium's core verb was a single call: worktree + branch + agent + first
# prompt. herdr has every one of those primitives, just uncomposed, so hspawn
# is the composition and nothing more:
#
#   herdr worktree create  -> a worktree workspace on <prefix>/<slug> (prefix =
#                             $HSPAWN_BRANCH_PREFIX, default $USER), and with it
#                             the worktree.created hooks (tdi.worktree-setup
#                             links node_modules et al, reviewr auto-opens)
#   herdr agent start      -> claude in that workspace's root pane
#   herdr agent prompt     -> the first instruction, waited on
#
# Usage:
#   hspawn [-p|--profile <clauth-profile>] [--tenant <tenant>] [-m|--model <model>] [-e|--effort <level>]
#          [--mode <auto|acceptEdits|plan|dontAsk|default>] [-b|--base <ref>] [-n|--name <agent-name>]
#          [--] <repo-dir> <slug> [prompt...]
#
#   hspawn ~/work/api fix-auth
#   hspawn -p personal ~/work/api fix-auth "Read AGENTS.md, then fix the 401 loop."
#   hspawn -m opus -e high --mode acceptEdits -b main ~/work/api fix-auth "Start with the tests."
#
# Option parsing stops at the first positional, so the prompt may contain
# anything (`hspawn repo slug Fix the -v flag` is fine). The legacy positional
# form — `hspawn <repo> <slug> [profile|-] [prompt...]` — keeps working for one
# release: without -p, a third positional that is `-` or the name of a
# registered clauth profile (a directory under ~/.clauth/profiles/, the layout
# on this machine 2026-08-30) is taken as the profile slot, with a deprecation
# note on stderr.
#
# --model / --effort / --mode reach claude on BOTH start paths: after `--` for
# `herdr agent start`, appended to the pane command for the isolated path.
# --mode defaults to auto, today's behaviour. `default` is accepted as a spelling
# of what `claude --help` (2.1.x) lists as `manual`; the CLI has no mode literally
# called "default". `dontAsk` (also 2.1.x) is accepted. `bypassPermissions` is
# refused ON PURPOSE: hspawn spawns run unattended, and unattended plus every
# permission gate off is not a combination this helper hands out.
#
# WHY A PROFILE CHANGES THE START PATH:
#   `herdr agent start` launches the agent binary herdr knows about, and gives it
#   no chance to inherit a CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. So the isolated path types a command
#   into the pane instead — `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=<account dir> claude ...`, which
#   the pane's own shell resolves to the claude() wrapper above — and then waits
#   for herdr to *detect* the claude underneath it, which is what `agent wait` is
#   for. The --shared path is the direct one, and burns whatever account owns the
#   global credential.
#
# WHY A SPAWN IS ISOLATED BY DEFAULT (2026-09-06):
#   ~/.claude/.credentials.json holds the login AND every MCP server's OAuth
#   token, at 0600, with NO lock — every refresh rewrites the whole file, and a
#   `clauth <profile>` switch rewrites it wholesale under every live session. That
#   is the mechanism behind the random logouts: of nine login-expiry incidents in
#   the eight days to 2026-09-06, five were SIMULTANEOUS across 3 to 6 sessions,
#   which is one bad value in one shared file rather than holders orphaning each
#   other. Every spawn on the shared credential is one more member of that group.
#
#   THE COST USED TO BE REAL AND IS NOT ANY MORE. Until 2026-09-06 the isolated
#   path ran `clauth start <profile>`, which launches the claude BINARY, never
#   reaches the claude() wrapper, and therefore had no teammateMode:tmux and could
#   not lead a herdr team; `--shared` existed as the escape hatch for a worker that
#   had to. It now points CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR at a persistent account dir and calls
#   `claude`, so a spawn is isolated AND can lead. `--shared` still means what it
#   says — use the global credential — and is now needed only when there is no
#   clauth profile to use.
#
#   Both paths print which credential they took, because nothing about the running
#   pane reveals it afterwards.
#
# Every agent call targets the PANE ID, not a name: herdr accepts either. The
# direct path names the agent at start; the profile path names it afterwards
# with `herdr agent rename <pane> <name>` (target first — checked against a
# nonexistent target, 2026-08-30), so both paths end with a named agent.
#
# TWO RACES right after `worktree create` (both observed live, 2026-08-30):
#   1. the new pane's shell has not reached its prompt yet — tens of seconds on
#      a box that is swapping — so `agent start` fails with agent_pane_busy
#      ("not an available shell") and text typed by `pane run` is buffered at
#      best, lost at worst. _hspawn_await_shell runs first, on both paths.
#   2. a worktree.created plugin hook may briefly occupy the pane afterwards, so
#      agent_pane_busy from `agent start` is retried (10x, 2 s apart); any other
#      error code fails at once with herdr's message.
#
# REGISTRY — the part Atrium had and herdr does not:
#   Every spawn is recorded in $HSPAWN_STATE_DIR (default ~/.local/state/hspawn/)
#   as <pane-id with ':' -> '_'>.json holding pane_id, workspace_id, branch,
#   slug, repo, profile, name, path, created_at and creator_session (the
#   $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID of the shell that ran hspawn, or ""). It is written
#   as soon as the worktree exists — before detection — so a spawn that dies
#   half-way is still hdespawn-able. hdespawn resolves slugs through it, and it
#   is the only thing that entitles hreap --close to touch a pane. hreap --close
#   annotates an entry (closed_at/closed_by) rather than deleting it, so that
#   hdespawn can still finish the teardown; hdespawn is what removes entries.
#   Nothing else writes there.
#
#   A PANE ID IS NOT A PERMANENT NAME. herdr's workspace ids come from a short
#   alphabet whose counter lives only in the running server, so a restart
#   reissues them (w4, w5, wN and wP were each created twice in five days in
#   this box's herdr-server.log) and each new workspace's first pane is p1 —
#   while the registry is a file that outlives every restart and deliberately
#   keeps entries around. So the key collides, and hspawn preserves rather than
#   overwrites (_hspawn_preserve_stale_registry). It also means `hdespawn
#   <pane-id>` is the exact form and `hdespawn <slug>` can go ambiguous, which
#   is why every "finish it with" line printed by this family names the pane.
#
#   hdespawn <slug|pane-id> [--yes]                  tear one spawn down
#   hreap [--close] [--mine] [--older N] [--json]    every Claude process herdr
#                                                    hosts, detected or not
#
# These are isolated single-account workers — the whole point of the worktree —
# so deliberately no teammux shim and no team lead. Use `claude` for that.

# Registry location; one JSON file per spawned pane (see header).
_hspawn_state_dir() {
  print -r -- "${HSPAWN_STATE_DIR:-${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/hspawn}"
}

# Registry file for a pane id: "wL:p1" -> ".../wL_p1.json".
_hspawn_registry_file() {
  print -r -- "$(_hspawn_state_dir)/${1//:/_}.json"
}

# Move an existing registry entry aside before hspawn writes a new one over it.
# Returns 0 when there was nothing to preserve or the move succeeded, 1 when an
# entry is there and could NOT be moved — the caller then declines to write, so
# the failure costs the new spawn's tracking (loud, and the pane is in front of
# you) rather than the old spawn's worktree (silent, and unrecoverable).
#
# Why this is needed: THE REGISTRY IS KEYED ON A PANE ID, AND A PANE ID IS NOT
# UNIQUE OVER TIME. herdr draws workspace ids from a short base-32 alphabet
# whose allocator lives only in the running server — ~/.config/herdr/session.json
# persists the workspaces themselves but no next-id counter — so a server
# restart reissues them from the start, and each new workspace's first pane is
# always p1. This box's own herdr-server.log has four instances in five days:
# w4 created 2026-08-28 and again 2026-08-29, w5 twice on 08-29, wN and wP on
# 08-30 and again on 08-31. So "wN:p1" has already been two different panes.
#
# The registry, by contrast, is a file under ~/.local/state and outlives every
# restart, and entries are deliberately long-lived: every hspawn bail-out says
# "registry entry kept", and `hreap --close` annotates rather than deletes so
# `hdespawn` can finish the teardown later. A bare `>` therefore destroys one of
# those entries without a word, and its worktree and branch are then orphaned
# with nothing on disk pointing at them — the exact failure hdespawn exists to
# prevent. hreap and hdespawn both already reason about id reuse; hspawn was the
# one of the three that did not.
#
# Renamed, not deleted, and kept in the same directory with a .json suffix so
# hdespawn's slug scan still finds it: `hdespawn <slug>` remains the way to
# finish it. If that slug is no longer unique the scan refuses and names the
# candidate FILES, which is why that listing prints the path.
_hspawn_preserve_stale_registry() {
  local registry="$1" archive stale_slug stale_branch stale_path stamp n=1
  [[ -f "$registry" ]] || return 0
  stale_slug="$(jq -r '.slug // empty'   "$registry" 2>/dev/null)"
  stale_branch="$(jq -r '.branch // empty' "$registry" 2>/dev/null)"
  stale_path="$(jq -r '.path // empty'   "$registry" 2>/dev/null)"
  stamp="$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)"
  archive="${registry%.json}.stale-$stamp.json"
  # Two spawns into the same reused id within one second is absurd and would
  # still lose an entry, so it is handled rather than assumed away.
  while [[ -e "$archive" ]]; do
    archive="${registry%.json}.stale-$stamp-$n.json"
    (( n++ ))
  done
  if command mv -- "$registry" "$archive" 2>/dev/null && [[ ! -e "$registry" ]]; then
    _hspawn_say warn "pane id reused: $registry already held an un-torn-down spawn" >&2
    _hspawn_say warn "  kept as $archive" >&2
    # The SLUG here, not the pane id. Both files now carry this pane id, and
    # hdespawn resolves a pane id to the live `<pane>.json`, so the pane form would
    # finish the NEW spawn rather than this archived one. The slug is what
    # distinguishes them — except when the same slug was respawned into the reused
    # id, where hdespawn refuses with "matches N spawns" and lists the files. The
    # archive path is printed above for exactly that case.
    _hspawn_say warn "  it is '${stale_slug:-?}'${stale_branch:+ ($stale_branch)}${stale_path:+ at $stale_path}${stale_slug:+ — finish it with: hdespawn $stale_slug}" >&2
    _hspawn_say warn "  (if that reports 'matches 2 spawns', this entry is the file named above)" >&2
    return 0
  fi
  _hspawn_say warn "could not move the existing $registry aside; NOT overwriting it" >&2
  return 1
}

# Aligned `key:       value` progress lines. printf is a builtin, so this is
# immune to the cat/ls aliases that zshrc.conditionals installs before this
# file is sourced (aliases expand when a function is *defined*).
_hspawn_say() {
  printf '%-10s %s\n' "$1:" "$2"
}

_hspawn_usage() {
  printf '%s\n' \
    'Usage: hspawn [options] [--] <repo-dir> <slug> [prompt...]' \
    '' \
    'Options:' \
    '  -p, --profile <clauth-profile>  run in that profile'"'"'s own persistent CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR' \
    '                                  (default: the clauth profile with the fewest live sessions).' \
    '                                  Isolated AND able to lead a herdr team.' \
    '      --shared                    use the SHARED global credential instead — adds a writer to the' \
    '                                  unlocked ~/.claude/.credentials.json, which a profile switch' \
    '                                  overwrites under every holder at once. Needed only when there is' \
    '                                  no clauth profile to use. See `claude-doctor`.' \
    '      --tenant <tenant>           bill this spawn to a named tenant pool, instead of the one' \
    '                                  the spawn directory resolves to' \
    '  -m, --model <model>             claude --model (an alias like opus, or a full model name)' \
    '  -e, --effort <level>            claude --effort (low|medium|high|xhigh|max)' \
    '      --mode <mode>               claude --permission-mode: auto (default) | acceptEdits | plan |' \
    '                                  dontAsk | default, a.k.a. manual (claude 2.1.x'"'"'s own name for' \
    '                                  it; both spellings are accepted).' \
    '                                  bypassPermissions is refused: hspawn spawns run unattended.' \
    '  -b, --base <ref>                start the worktree branch from <ref> (default: herdr'"'"'s own)' \
    '  -n, --name <agent-name>         herdr agent name (default: <slug> folded to [a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31})' \
    '  -h, --help' \
    '' \
    'Deprecated (kept one release): hspawn <repo-dir> <slug> [profile|-] [prompt...]' \
    '  (not after `--`: that turns the slot off, so a literal `-` stays in the prompt)' \
    '' \
    'Examples:' \
    '  hspawn ~/work/api fix-auth' \
    '  hspawn -p personal ~/work/api fix-auth "Read AGENTS.md, then fix the 401 loop."' \
    '  hspawn -m opus -e high --mode acceptEdits -b main ~/work/api fix-auth "Start with the tests."' \
    '' \
    "Registry: $(_hspawn_state_dir)   Twins: hdespawn <slug|pane-id>, hreap [--close]" >&2
}

# Answer Claude Code's trust-folder dialog in a freshly created worktree pane.
#
# Must run AFTER the agent is detected: this uses the agent surface, which is
# what actually delivers the keys. Sending them at the pane level the moment the
# dialog text renders does NOT work — Claude's TUI is not accepting input yet,
# the keys are swallowed, and the dialog stays up with no error (verified
# 2026-08-30).
#
# The dialog defaults to "No, exit", so the answer is: down, then enter.
# Returns 0 when there is nothing to answer, which is the common case for a
# worktree under an already-trusted parent.
_hspawn_clear_trust_dialog() {
  local pane="$1" attempt state
  for attempt in 1 2 3; do
    state="$(herdr agent get "$pane" 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.result.agent.agent_status // empty')"
    [[ "$state" == "blocked" ]] || return 0
    herdr agent read "$pane" --source detection --lines 30 2>/dev/null \
      | grep -q "Yes, I trust this folder" || return 0
    herdr agent send-keys "$pane" down  >/dev/null 2>&1
    herdr agent send-keys "$pane" enter >/dev/null 2>&1
    herdr agent wait "$pane" --until idle --timeout 15000 >/dev/null 2>&1
  done
  _hspawn_say warn "trust dialog still up after 3 attempts" >&2
  return 1
}

# Is the pane's shell at its prompt? Reads ONE `pane process-info` reply (as
# $1) and prints true/false. Split out of the poll loop below so it can be
# tested against recorded replies — scripts/test-hspawn.sh.
#
# The pane is at its prompt exactly when the tty's FOREGROUND PROCESS GROUP is
# the shell itself. herdr publishes both halves of that comparison —
# `shell_pid` and `foreground_process_group_id`, both declared fields of
# ProcessInfo in `herdr api schema --json` — and probed across every live pane
# on this box (2026-09-01, herdr 0.8.2) the two are EQUAL in each pane sitting
# at a zsh prompt and DIFFER in each pane running Claude. Nothing else
# separates the two states as cleanly.
#
# It replaced "exactly one foreground process, and its name is one of zsh,
# bash, sh, fish, dash". The name list is the defect: a pane whose shell is not
# one of those five never reads ready, so hspawn aborts at its 60 s timeout
# with the worktree, the workspace and the registry entry ALREADY created —
# and $SHELL was never consulted, with no override to reach for. (The
# `length == 1` half read as a second defect and is not one: the field is the
# foreground process GROUP, so a second entry appears only while the shell is
# BUSY running a pipeline, which is correctly not-ready. Recorded here so it is
# not "fixed" later by loosening the test to `any`, which would report ready
# while a command is still running.)
#
# The name test survives only as a fallback for a herdr that publishes neither
# pid, and it now includes $SHELL's own basename.
_hspawn_shell_ready() {
  local -a shell_names=(zsh bash sh fish dash)
  [[ -n "${SHELL:t}" ]] && shell_names+=("${SHELL:t}")
  # Space-delimited membership rather than jq --args: one string argument works
  # on every jq this repo has run against, and a shell basename cannot contain
  # a space.
  printf '%s' "$1" | jq -r --arg shells " ${(j: :)shell_names} " '
    (.result.process_info // {}) as $pi
    | if ($pi.shell_pid // null) != null and ($pi.foreground_process_group_id // null) != null
      then $pi.foreground_process_group_id == $pi.shell_pid
      else (($pi.foreground_processes // []) | length) == 1
           and ($shells | contains(" " + (($pi.foreground_processes // [])[0].name // "") + " "))
      end' 2>/dev/null
}

# Poll until the pane's shell is at its prompt (see _hspawn_shell_ready). Right
# after `worktree create` that is not yet true (see the header), and
# `agent start` then fails with agent_pane_busy while `pane run` text goes
# nowhere. Prints the seconds waited on success; on timeout prints what it last
# saw — the foreground names AND both pids, because with the pid test in front
# the names alone no longer explain a refusal.
_hspawn_await_shell() {
  local pane="$1" limit="${2:-60}" waited=0 info ready names pids
  while :; do
    info="$(herdr pane process-info --pane "$pane" 2>/dev/null)"
    ready="$(_hspawn_shell_ready "$info")"
    if [[ "$ready" == true ]]; then
      print -r -- "$waited"
      return 0
    fi
    (( waited >= limit )) && break
    sleep 1
    (( waited++ ))
  done
  names="$(printf '%s' "$info" | jq -r '[.result.process_info.foreground_processes[]?.name] | join(", ")' 2>/dev/null)"
  pids="$(printf '%s' "$info" | jq -r '.result.process_info
    | "fg pgid \(.foreground_process_group_id // "?"), shell pid \(.shell_pid // "?")"' 2>/dev/null)"
  echo "Error: pane $pane has not reached a shell prompt after ${limit}s (foreground: ${names:-none/unknown}${pids:+; $pids})" >&2
  return 1
}

# Poll until herdr has actually detected an agent in the pane. `agent wait`
# cannot do this: it resolves its target first and errors out if no agent is
# there yet.
_hspawn_await_agent() {
  local pane="$1" limit="${2:-120}" waited=0
  while (( waited < limit )); do
    herdr agent get "$pane" >/dev/null 2>&1 && return 0
    sleep 1
    (( waited++ ))
  done
  return 1
}

hspawn() {
  local profile="" profile_set=0 model="" effort="" mode="auto" base="" name_override=""
  local tenant="" tenant_set=0
  local endopts=0 shared=0
  # REPLY is how _claude_pick_profile answers, and it is a shell-wide name: local
  # here so a spawn cannot leave its choice behind in the calling shell.
  local REPLY=""
  local _claude_pick_load="" _claude_pick_overflow=0
  local _CLAUDE_TENANT="" _CLAUDE_TENANT_STATE="" _CLAUDE_TENANT_WHY=""
  local -a _claude_pick_skipped=() _claude_pick_warnings=()
  local -a _claude_pick_explain=() _claude_pick_exhausted=()
  local -a _claude_pick_cands=() _claude_pick_unknowns=()
  local -a _claude_pick_pool_members=() _claude_pick_overflow_members=()
  local _claude_pick_state="" _claude_pick_class="" _claude_pick_reason=""
  local _claude_pick_score="" _claude_pick_tenant="" _claude_pick_tenant_state=""
  local _claude_pick_u5=unknown _claude_pick_uw=unknown _claude_pick_r5=unknown
  local _claude_pick_rw=unknown _claude_pick_age=unknown _claude_pick_tier=unknown
  local _claude_pick_holders=0 _claude_pick_leastbad="" _claude_pick_leastbad_r5=unknown
  local _CML_LOAD1=unknown _CML_NCPU=unknown _CML_SWAPPCT=unknown
  local _claude_pick_prog=hspawn
  local -a rest=()
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      --shared)
        # Opt back into the shared global credential. See "WHY A SPAWN IS
        # ISOLATED BY DEFAULT" in the header: this is the flag for a worker that
        # has to LEAD a team, and it is the only way to get the old behaviour.
        shared=1
        shift
        continue ;;
      --profile=*|--model=*|--effort=*|--mode=*|--base=*|--name=*|--tenant=*)
        set -- "${1%%=*}" "${1#*=}" "${(@)argv[2,-1]}"
        continue ;;
      -h|--help)
        _hspawn_usage
        return 0 ;;
      --)
        # Recorded, because `--` also has to switch OFF the deprecated
        # positional [profile|-] slot below. That slot triggers on a literal
        # "-" in the third position, which is precisely the argument a user
        # writes `--` to protect: without this, `hspawn -- ~/repo slug - "…"`
        # ate the "-" and printed a deprecation note the user had just opted
        # out of, so `--` did not mean what it says.
        endopts=1
        shift
        rest=("$@")
        break ;;
      -p|--profile|-m|--model|-e|--effort|--mode|-b|--base|-n|--name|--tenant)
        # Empty as well as missing: `-p ''` / `--profile=` would otherwise set
        # profile_set with an empty profile, silently taking the no-profile path
        # (burning whatever account holds the global credentials) AND disabling
        # the legacy positional slot.
        if (( $# < 2 )) || [[ -z "$2" ]]; then
          echo "Error: $1 needs a non-empty value (hspawn --help)" >&2
          return 1
        fi
        case "$1" in
          -p|--profile) profile="$2"; profile_set=1 ;;
          -m|--model)   model="$2" ;;
          -e|--effort)  effort="$2" ;;
          --mode)       mode="$2" ;;
          -b|--base)    base="$2" ;;
          -n|--name)    name_override="$2" ;;
          --tenant)     tenant="$2"; tenant_set=1 ;;
        esac
        shift 2 ;;
      -?*)
        echo "Error: unknown option '$1' (hspawn --help)" >&2
        return 1 ;;
      *)
        # First positional: everything from here on is repo/slug/prompt.
        rest=("$@")
        break ;;
    esac
  done
  if (( ${#rest} < 2 )); then
    _hspawn_usage
    return 1
  fi
  if ! command -v jq &>/dev/null; then
    echo "Error: hspawn needs jq to read herdr's JSON replies" >&2
    return 1
  fi

  local repo="${rest[1]}" slug="${rest[2]}"
  local -a prompt_words=("${(@)rest[3,-1]}")
  # Legacy positional form (see header): [profile|-] in the third slot.
  # Never after an explicit `--` — see the endopts note in the parser.
  if (( ! profile_set && ! endopts && ${#rest} >= 3 )); then
    local third="${rest[3]}"
    if [[ "$third" == "-" || -d "$HOME/.clauth/profiles/$third" ]]; then
      # A literal "-" was always "no profile", i.e. the shared global credential.
      # It keeps meaning that, so the legacy form does not silently acquire the
      # new isolated default.
      if [[ "$third" == "-" ]]; then shared=1; else profile="$third"; fi
      prompt_words=("${(@)rest[4,-1]}")
      echo "deprecated: the positional [profile|-] slot; use -p/--profile <profile> (or just omit it)" >&2
    fi
  fi
  local prompt="${(j: :)prompt_words}"

  if [[ ! -d "$repo" ]]; then
    echo "Error: '$repo' is not a directory" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  repo="${repo:a}"

  case "$mode" in
    default) mode="manual" ;;
    auto|acceptEdits|plan|manual|dontAsk) ;;
    bypassPermissions)
      echo "Error: --mode bypassPermissions is refused on purpose: hspawn spawns run unattended," >&2
      echo "       and an unattended agent with every permission gate off is not something this" >&2
      echo "       helper hands out. Use --mode dontAsk, or start the pane by hand." >&2
      return 1 ;;
    *)
      # `manual` is listed here because the validator accepts it: it is what
      # claude 2.1.x actually calls the mode, `default` is only rewritten to it
      # above, and an error message that omits a value it would have accepted
      # sends the user to re-read --help for a mode that was already right.
      echo "Error: --mode must be auto, acceptEdits, plan, dontAsk, manual or default (got '$mode')" >&2
      return 1 ;;
  esac

  # Agent names are constrained to [a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}. Only the name is
  # folded down to fit — the branch and label keep the slug as typed, so an
  # uppercase Linear id stays readable where you actually read it.
  local name
  if [[ -n "$name_override" ]]; then
    name="$name_override"
    if [[ ${#name} -gt 32 || "$name" != [a-z]* || -n "${name//[a-z0-9_-]/}" ]]; then
      echo "Error: --name must match [a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31} (got '$name')" >&2
      return 1
    fi
  else
    name="${(L)slug}"
    name="${name//[^a-z0-9_-]/-}"
    [[ "$name" == [a-z]* ]] || name="w-$name"
    name="${name[1,32]}"
  fi

  # ---- Isolate the spawn's credentials unless told otherwise ----------------
  # See "WHY A SPAWN IS ISOLATED BY DEFAULT" in the header. Without a profile a
  # spawn runs on the SHARED ~/.claude/.credentials.json, which has no lock, is
  # rewritten in full on every token refresh, and is replaced wholesale by a
  # `clauth <profile>` switch — five of the nine login-expiry incidents in the
  # eight days to 2026-09-06 took 3 to 6 sessions down at the same instant.
  #
  # PLACED AFTER every argument check, so an invocation that is about to be
  # refused does not fork clauth first. That is not only tidiness: several rows
  # of the state table assert that a rejected hspawn reached NOTHING, and a
  # resolution step in front of the validation makes them false.
  #
  # NEVER SILENT, in either direction: which credential a spawn burns decides
  # which account is billed, so both paths say so.
  if (( ! profile_set )) && [[ -z "$profile" ]] && (( ! shared )); then
    if command -v clauth >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      # NOT `clauth which`. That answers from $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, so inside an
      # isolated pane it returns THIS session's own profile — measured
      # 2026-09-06, it names the parent session's profile in an isolated shell
      # and `unknown` in a bare one. Defaulting to it puts every spawn in the
      # same credential group as its parent, which is the concentration this is
      # meant to undo.
      # Called DIRECTLY, never as `$(...)`: the picker reports through globals
      # (the score that won, every account it refused, the machine's load) and a
      # subshell would discard them — and with them the reason a spawn landed
      # where it did.
      # Resolve the tenant for the directory being SPAWNED INTO, not for the
      # lead's $PWD: a lead standing in one tree routinely spawns workers into
      # another, and billing the worker to the lead's tenant is the same
      # wrong-account bug one level down. `$repo` is handed to the picker, which
      # resolves it — unless --tenant or CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_TENANT pinned one.
      if (( ! tenant_set )) && [[ -n "${CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_TENANT:-}" ]]; then
        tenant="$CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_TENANT"
      fi
      # STRICT. hspawn is a headless caller (§5.4): a worker starting on a spent
      # 5h window burns the seat and dies mid-task with nobody watching, so an
      # exhausted pool refuses here rather than proceeding on the least-bad
      # member the way an interactive `claude` does. The escape is `-p <profile>`,
      # which the refusal names, and the reset time it prints says how long the
      # wait is.
      _claude_pick_for_dir "$repo" "$tenant" 1 0
      local pick_rc=$?
      profile="$REPLY"
      tenant="$_claude_pick_tenant"
      local _w
      for _w in "${_claude_pick_warnings[@]}"; do echo "hspawn: $_w" >&2; done
      if (( pick_rc == 2 || pick_rc == 3 )); then
        # The picker already printed the §5.4 block on stderr for rc 2. Say what
        # it means for THIS command — that nothing was created — because an
        # exhaustion report on its own reads like a warning.
        (( pick_rc == 3 )) && echo "hspawn: refused — $_claude_pick_reason" >&2
        echo "hspawn: nothing was spawned. Retry, or pass -p <profile> to choose an account" >&2
        echo "        deliberately." >&2
        return 1
      fi
      if [[ -z "$profile" ]]; then
        # Could not name an account. Falling through to the shared credential is
        # the right choice — it is the old behaviour, and inventing a profile
        # would bill an account the user did not pick — but it must be REPORTED,
        # because the caller asked for isolation by omission and is not getting
        # it, and nothing about the running pane reveals that afterwards.
        shared=1
        echo "hspawn: clauth is installed but no profile has a credential — this spawn shares the" >&2
        echo "        global credential (no isolation). 'clauth login <name>', or pass -p <profile>." >&2
      fi
    else
      shared=1
    fi
  fi

  # The account dir is built HERE, before a worktree, a pane or a registry entry
  # exists. A spawn whose credential cannot be prepared should cost nothing to
  # clean up — the same reasoning that put the resolution above after argument
  # validation.
  local account_dir=""
  if [[ -n "$profile" ]]; then
    if [[ ! -x "$(_claude_account_builder)" ]]; then
      echo "hspawn: ${$(_claude_account_builder)/#$HOME/~} is not available, so this spawn cannot be" >&2
      echo "        isolated. Sharing the global credential instead." >&2
      profile=""; shared=1
    elif ! account_dir="$(command "$(_claude_account_builder)" "$profile")" || [[ -z "$account_dir" ]]; then
      echo "Error: could not build an account dir for clauth profile '$profile' (reason above)." >&2
      echo "       Fix it, name another with -p, or pass --shared to use the global credential." >&2
      return 1
    fi
  fi

  # Claude's own args — identical on both start paths.
  local -a claude_args=(--permission-mode "$mode")
  [[ -n "$model" ]]  && claude_args+=(--model "$model")
  [[ -n "$effort" ]] && claude_args+=(--effort "$effort")

  # Branch prefix follows the repo's <person>/<topic> convention. Defaults to
  # $USER rather than a hardcoded name — this file is shared with the team.
  local branch_prefix="${HSPAWN_BRANCH_PREFIX:-$USER}"
  local branch="$branch_prefix/$slug"
  local -a create_args=(--cwd "$repo" --branch "$branch" --label "$slug" --no-focus)
  [[ -n "$base" ]] && create_args+=(--base "$base")

  local created pane workspace wt_path
  # herdr prints its error JSON on stderr, so a failure is already visible.
  created="$(herdr worktree create "${create_args[@]}")" || {
    echo "Error: herdr worktree create failed (its reply is above)" >&2
    return 1
  }
  pane="$(printf '%s' "$created" | jq -r '.result.root_pane.pane_id // empty')"
  if [[ -z "$pane" ]]; then
    echo "Error: no .result.root_pane.pane_id in the worktree create reply:" >&2
    printf '%s\n' "$created" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  # Reply shape per the herdr 0.8.2 API schema (`herdr api schema --json`):
  # {workspace: WorkspaceInfo, tab, root_pane: PaneInfo, worktree: WorktreeInfo}.
  # The pane id's prefix is the workspace id, kept as a fallback.
  workspace="$(printf '%s' "$created" | jq -r '.result.workspace.workspace_id // empty')"
  [[ -n "$workspace" ]] || workspace="${pane%%:*}"
  wt_path="$(printf '%s' "$created" | jq -r '.result.worktree.path // empty')"
  _hspawn_say worktree "$branch${wt_path:+  ($wt_path)}"
  _hspawn_say base "${base:-(herdr default)}"
  _hspawn_say pane "$pane"
  _hspawn_say workspace "$workspace"
  # Which credential this spawn burns, and what that costs it. Stated on both
  # paths: it decides which account is billed AND whether the worker can lead a
  # herdr team, and neither is recoverable by looking at the pane afterwards.
  if [[ -n "$profile" ]]; then
    _hspawn_say account "clauth profile '$profile' (isolated; can lead a team)"
  else
    _hspawn_say account "SHARED global credential (one more holder of ~/.claude/.credentials.json)"
  fi

  # Registry entry — written now, not after detection (see header).
  local state_dir registry
  local registry_failed=0
  state_dir="$(_hspawn_state_dir)"
  registry="$(_hspawn_registry_file "$pane")"
  if mkdir -p "$state_dir" && _hspawn_preserve_stale_registry "$registry" && jq -n \
      --arg pane_id "$pane" --arg workspace_id "$workspace" --arg branch "$branch" \
      --arg slug "$slug" --arg repo "$repo" --arg profile "$profile" --arg name "$name" \
      --arg path "$wt_path" --arg created_at "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" \
      --arg creator_session "${CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID:-}" \
      '$ARGS.named' > "$registry"; then
    _hspawn_say registry "$registry"
  else
    # The spawn CONTINUES — the pane and the agent are usable, and discarding them
    # over a bookkeeping failure would be worse. But it must not report SUCCESS:
    # with no registry entry, hdespawn and hreap cannot see this worktree at all,
    # so it is a leak on a box where memory is the binding constraint, and
    # `hspawn … && echo ok` printed ok. The status is carried to the end of the
    # function rather than returned here.
    registry_failed=1
    _hspawn_say warn "could not write $registry — hdespawn/hreap will not know this spawn" >&2
    _hspawn_say warn "  finish it by hand: herdr worktree remove --workspace $workspace --force" >&2
  fi

  # Race 1 (header): the pane's shell is usually not at its prompt yet.
  #
  # Every bail-out from here down names the PANE ID rather than the slug, for
  # the reason spelled out at hreap's "Finish with:" line: the registry file is
  # keyed on the pane id, so that lookup is exact, while `hdespawn <slug>` scans
  # and REFUSES when two entries share the slug. These messages are printed in
  # precisely the state that manufactures a duplicate — a spawn that failed and
  # left its entry behind, then retried under the same slug.
  local shell_wait
  shell_wait="$(_hspawn_await_shell "$pane" 60)" || {
    echo "       (registry entry kept; \`hdespawn $pane\` tears the worktree down)" >&2
    return 1
  }
  _hspawn_say shell "at prompt after ${shell_wait}s"

  if [[ -n "$profile" ]]; then
    # Quote each word for the shell that `pane run` types into. (@q) is required:
    # inside double quotes zsh joins an array BEFORE applying (q), which would
    # yield one backslash-escaped word (`--permission-mode\ auto`); (@) keeps the
    # elements separate so each is quoted on its own, then (j: :) joins them.
    # `claude`, not the claude BINARY: the pane's own shell resolves it to the
    # claude() wrapper at the top of this file, which is what supplies
    # teammateMode:tmux. CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR is a prefix assignment on a FUNCTION
    # call, and zsh exports those into the function's children — verified, and the
    # single fact this whole path rests on.
    herdr pane run "$pane" "CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=${(q)account_dir} claude ${(j: :)${(@q)claude_args}}" || return 1
  else
    # `agent start` fails in three distinguishable ways (error JSON on stderr):
    #   agent_not_ready — the agent came up blocked (the trust dialog below all
    #                     but guarantees it) but IS registered: not fatal;
    #                     _hspawn_await_agent decides whether anything started.
    #   agent_pane_busy — race 2 (header): the pane is "not an available shell".
    #                     Retry up to 10x, 2 s apart, then give up.
    #   anything else   — fail now, with herdr's message.
    local attempt reply code msg
    for attempt in {1..11}; do
      if reply="$(herdr agent start "$name" --kind claude --pane "$pane" -- "${claude_args[@]}" 2>&1)"; then
        break
      fi
      # herdr puts one JSON object on stderr; tolerate any other lines around it.
      code="$(printf '%s\n' "$reply" | jq -rRs '[split("\n")[] | fromjson? | .error.code // empty] | last // empty' 2>/dev/null)"
      msg="$(printf '%s\n' "$reply" | jq -rRs '[split("\n")[] | fromjson? | .error.message // empty] | last // empty' 2>/dev/null)"
      [[ -n "$msg" ]] || msg="$reply"
      case "$code" in
        agent_not_ready)
          _hspawn_say note "agent start reported agent_not_ready (expected if the trust dialog is up)"
          break ;;
        agent_pane_busy)
          if (( attempt > 10 )); then
            echo "Error: pane $pane still busy after 10 retries — herdr agent start: $msg" >&2
            echo "       (registry entry kept; \`hdespawn $pane\` tears the worktree down)" >&2
            return 1
          fi
          _hspawn_say note "pane busy (agent_pane_busy) — retry $attempt/10 in 2s"
          sleep 2 ;;
        *)
          echo "Error: herdr agent start failed${code:+ ($code)}: $msg" >&2
          echo "       (registry entry kept; \`hdespawn $pane\` tears the worktree down)" >&2
          return 1 ;;
      esac
    done
  fi

  _hspawn_await_agent "$pane" 120 || {
    echo "Error: no agent detected in $pane after 120s — check the pane" >&2
    echo "       (registry entry kept; \`hdespawn $pane\` tears the worktree down)" >&2
    return 1
  }
  herdr agent wait "$pane" --until idle --until blocked --timeout 180000 >/dev/null || return 1

  # EVERY hspawn run creates a brand-new worktree directory, so Claude's
  # trust-folder dialog is effectively guaranteed and its default is "No, exit".
  # The status has to propagate. The helper already warns on stderr after three
  # attempts, but without this hspawn returned 0 and printed its success lines,
  # leaving a pane parked on that dialog: absent from `herdr agent list`, the
  # sidebar and the priority sort, and holding ~400MB where memory is the binding
  # constraint. The blocked re-check below cannot cover it — that one lives inside
  # `if [[ -n "$prompt" ]]`, so it only guards the with-prompt path.
  if ! _hspawn_clear_trust_dialog "$pane"; then
    # Every bail-out from here down names the pane id; this one printed nothing at
    # all, leaving the worktree, workspace, pane and registry entry with no stated
    # way to finish them.
    echo "       (registry entry kept; \`hdespawn $pane\` tears the worktree down)" >&2
    return 1
  fi

  if [[ -n "$profile" ]]; then
    # The pane command started the agent, so herdr never got to name it at
    # `agent start` time. Name it now.
    herdr agent rename "$pane" "$name" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || _hspawn_say note "rename to '$name' failed (name taken?) — address the agent as $pane"
  fi
  _hspawn_say agent "$name in $pane${profile:+ (clauth profile '$profile')}"

  if [[ -n "$prompt" ]]; then
    # herdr refuses to type into an agent sitting at an approval dialog, so say
    # so plainly rather than emitting a bare agent_blocked error.
    local state
    state="$(herdr agent get "$pane" 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.result.agent.agent_status // empty')"
    if [[ "$state" == "blocked" ]]; then
      echo "Error: agent is blocked (unanswered dialog); prompt not sent." >&2
      echo "       Inspect with: herdr agent read $pane --source detection" >&2
      return 1
    fi
    herdr agent prompt "$pane" "$prompt" --wait || return 1
  fi

  _hspawn_say next "herdr agent read $pane · hreap · hdespawn $slug"
  # Non-zero when the registry entry could not be written: the spawn worked, but
  # nothing can find it afterwards, and a caller chaining on hspawn's status has to
  # know that. The manual teardown is printed with the warning above.
  (( registry_failed )) && return 1
  return 0
}

#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hdespawn — tear down one hspawn worktree, resolved through the registry
#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resolves <slug|pane-id> to its registry file, shows what is about to go —
# including what removal would DISCARD: uncommitted paths (`git status
# --porcelain`) and unpushed commits: commits @{upstream} lacks, or with no
# upstream (the normal state of a fresh hspawn branch) commits on NO origin
# branch at all (`rev-list HEAD --not --remotes=origin`) — so a worktree cut
# from a pushed feature branch counts 0, not that branch's lead over
# origin/main — asks, then removes the workspace and drops the registry
# file. Guard: without --yes the work summary is part of the question; with
# --yes it REFUSES to remove a workspace/worktree whose checkout is dirty,
# has such commits, or could not be inspected, unless --force is also given.
# (The user declined to kill Atrium sessions today for exactly this reason:
# unpushed commits.) The branch is left alone — deleting it is the user's
# call, so the command is printed, not run. A registry entry whose workspace
# is no longer in the session (closed by hand, by `hreap --close`, or a server
# restart) FINISHES the teardown herdr can no longer do: the same at-risk
# guard applies (--yes refuses, entry kept, unless --force), then
# `git worktree remove` (--force when discarding) takes the worktree dir, and
# only then does the entry go — so hreap's "Finish with: hdespawn <slug>"
# line is a promise, not a hope. With no worktree dir left on disk there is
# nothing to discard and the stale entry is simply dropped. If the workspace
# id now holds a different worktree than the entry recorded, nothing is
# touched at all.
hdespawn() {
  local yes=0 force=0 target=""
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      -y|--yes)   yes=1 ;;
      -f|--force) force=1 ;;
      -h|--help)
        echo "Usage: hdespawn [-y|--yes] [-f|--force] <slug|pane-id>" >&2
        echo "  --yes    skip the confirmation; still refuses to discard uncommitted/unpushed work" >&2
        echo "  --force  with --yes: discard that work anyway" >&2
        return 0 ;;
      -?*)
        echo "Error: unknown option '$1' (hdespawn --help)" >&2
        return 1 ;;
      *)
        # A destructive command silently acting on the LAST of two targets is
        # how the wrong worktree dies; exactly one <slug|pane-id> is accepted.
        if [[ -n "$target" ]]; then
          echo "Error: hdespawn takes exactly one <slug|pane-id> (got '$target' and '$1')" >&2
          return 1
        fi
        target="$1" ;;
    esac
    shift
  done
  if [[ -z "$target" ]]; then
    echo "Usage: hdespawn [-y|--yes] [-f|--force] <slug|pane-id>" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  if ! command -v jq &>/dev/null; then
    echo "Error: hdespawn needs jq to read the hspawn registry" >&2
    return 1
  fi

  local state_dir registry f
  state_dir="$(_hspawn_state_dir)"
  registry="$(_hspawn_registry_file "$target")"
  if [[ ! -f "$registry" ]]; then
    local -a hits=()
    for f in "$state_dir"/*.json(N); do
      [[ "$(jq -r '.slug // empty' "$f" 2>/dev/null)" == "$target" ]] && hits+=("$f")
    done
    case ${#hits} in
      0)
        echo "Error: no hspawn registry entry for '$target' (registry: $state_dir)" >&2
        return 1 ;;
      1) registry="${hits[1]}" ;;
      *)
        # The FILE is named too, not just the pane id: pane ids are reused (see
        # _hspawn_preserve_stale_registry), so two entries for one slug can
        # carry the SAME pane id — one live, one preserved from before a server
        # restart — and then the pane id disambiguates nothing. The path always
        # does, and it is what a human needs to finish the older one by hand.
        echo "Error: slug '$target' matches ${#hits} spawns — use the pane id:" >&2
        for f in "${hits[@]}"; do
          jq -r --arg f "$f" '"  \(.pane_id)  \(.repo)  \(.branch)   [\($f)]"' "$f" >&2
        done
        return 1 ;;
    esac
  fi

  # @sh single-quotes STRINGS only — a JSON array becomes several bare shell
  # words and a number/boolean one unquoted word, which eval would run — so
  # every field is forced through `tostring` first (an array/number collapses
  # to one quoted string). hspawn writes strings via --arg, but the registry
  # is just a file on disk; defence in depth. A jq failure (corrupt file)
  # leaves every field empty and the ws guard below rejects the entry.
  local pane="" ws="" branch="" repo="" slug="" wt_path="" reg_path="" created_at="" creator="" closed_at="" closed_by=""
  eval "$(jq -r '@sh "pane=\(.pane_id // "" | tostring) ws=\(.workspace_id // "" | tostring) branch=\(.branch // "" | tostring) repo=\(.repo // "" | tostring) slug=\(.slug // "" | tostring) wt_path=\(.path // "" | tostring) created_at=\(.created_at // "" | tostring) creator=\(.creator_session // "" | tostring) closed_at=\(.closed_at // "" | tostring) closed_by=\(.closed_by // "" | tostring)"' "$registry")"
  reg_path="$wt_path"
  if [[ -z "$ws" ]]; then
    echo "Error: $registry has no workspace_id — remove it by hand" >&2
    return 1
  fi

  # What herdr currently knows about it (the workspace may already be gone).
  local snapshot ws_live="" live_path="" agent_status=""
  snapshot="$(herdr api snapshot 2>/dev/null)"
  if [[ -n "$snapshot" ]]; then
    eval "$(printf '%s' "$snapshot" | jq -r --arg ws "$ws" --arg pane "$pane" '
      .result.snapshot as $s
      | (first($s.workspaces[]? | select(.workspace_id == $ws)) // {}) as $w
      | (first($s.panes[]? | select(.pane_id == $pane)) // {}) as $p
      | @sh "ws_live=\($w.workspace_id // "") live_path=\($w.worktree.checkout_path // "") agent_status=\($p.agent_status // "")"' 2>/dev/null)"
  fi
  # Same id, different worktree. No longer "UNVERIFIED": herdr DOES reuse
  # workspace ids — the allocator lives only in the running server, so a restart
  # reissues them from the start, and this box's server log shows w4, w5, wN and
  # wP each issued twice in five days (see _hspawn_preserve_stale_registry).
  # The registry outlives every restart, so an entry naming a workspace that now
  # belongs to somebody else's worktree is a state that happens.
  #
  # Refusing outright — what this did — is safe but dead-ends: the entry's own
  # worktree is still on disk and no command will ever finish it. So the live
  # workspace is DISOWNED instead, treated exactly like one that is gone, and
  # the teardown continues against the path THIS entry recorded, behind the same
  # uncommitted/unpushed guard. Nothing herdr owns is touched on that path: no
  # `worktree remove`, no pane close, no agent killed.
  # Fires whenever this entry cannot be PROVEN to own the live workspace, which is
  # two cases, not one:
  #   * the recorded path differs from the live one — the id was reused;
  #   * the entry recorded NO path at all. Not hypothetical: that is precisely the
  #     state the recovery below exists for. Requiring a non-empty reg_path meant
  #     such an entry skipped the disown, adopted the live workspace's checkout as
  #     its own two lines down, and then tore it down — `herdr worktree remove
  #     --workspace` would have removed ANOTHER spawn's worktree and closed their
  #     pane. The branch lookups below identify this entry's worktree without
  #     guessing, so falling through to them is both safe and correct.
  if [[ -n "$ws_live" && -n "$live_path" ]] && [[ -z "$reg_path" || "$live_path" != "$reg_path" ]]; then
    if [[ -z "$reg_path" ]]; then
      _hspawn_say note "this entry recorded no worktree path, so workspace $ws ($live_path) cannot be shown to be its own"
      _hspawn_say note "  leaving that workspace alone; finishing this entry from its branch"
    else
      _hspawn_say note "workspace $ws now holds $live_path, not this entry's $reg_path"
      _hspawn_say note "  the id was reused — leaving that workspace alone; finishing this entry from disk"
    fi
    ws_live="" live_path="" agent_status=""
  fi
  [[ -n "$live_path" ]] && wt_path="$live_path"
  # NOT gated on $ws_live. After `hreap --close` the workspace is gone, and that is
  # exactly the state this recovery exists for — gating it on a live workspace made
  # it unreachable there, so hdespawn printed "no worktree dir on disk — nothing to
  # remove", removed the registry entry anyway, and left the worktree and its branch
  # orphaned with nothing pointing at them. The opposite of this function's promise.
  if [[ -z "$wt_path" ]]; then
    wt_path="$(herdr worktree list --cwd "$repo" 2>/dev/null \
      | jq -r --arg b "$branch" 'first(.result.worktrees[]? | select(.branch == $b) | .path) // empty')"
  fi
  # And git knows even when herdr does not: the worktree outlives the workspace,
  # which is the whole reason an entry can be stranded in the first place.
  if [[ -z "$wt_path" ]]; then
    wt_path="$(git -C "$repo" worktree list --porcelain 2>/dev/null \
      | awk -v b="refs/heads/$branch" '
          /^worktree /{ p = substr($0, 10) }
          /^branch /  { if (substr($0, 8) == b) { print p; exit } }')"
  fi

  local ws_note="not in the current herdr session"
  [[ -n "$ws_live" ]] && ws_note="open"
  echo "hdespawn: $slug"
  _hspawn_say pane "$pane${agent_status:+ (agent $agent_status)}"
  _hspawn_say workspace "$ws ($ws_note)"
  _hspawn_say branch "$branch"
  _hspawn_say worktree "${wt_path:-(path unknown to herdr)}"

  # What removal would discard. dirty = paths in `git status --porcelain`
  # (untracked included); unpushed = commits on HEAD that @{upstream} lacks
  # or, with no upstream (the normal state of a fresh hspawn branch), commits
  # on NO origin branch at all. Comparing against origin/HEAD or origin/main
  # here overcounted: a worktree cut from a pushed feature branch carries that
  # branch's lead over main, and --yes refused over commits that were in fact
  # on origin/<feature> (observed 2026-08-31). In a repo with no origin every
  # commit counts — alarming but fail-safe (at_risk, not silent removal).
  local dirty="" unpushed="" unpushed_label="unpushed" work="" at_risk=0
  if [[ -n "$wt_path" && -d "$wt_path" ]] \
     && command git -C "$wt_path" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    dirty="$(command git -C "$wt_path" status --porcelain 2>/dev/null | command wc -l)"
    dirty="${dirty// /}"
    local upstream=""
    if upstream="$(command git -C "$wt_path" rev-parse --abbrev-ref '@{upstream}' 2>/dev/null)"; then
      unpushed="$(command git -C "$wt_path" rev-list --count "$upstream..HEAD" 2>/dev/null)"
      unpushed_label="not on $upstream"
    else
      unpushed="$(command git -C "$wt_path" rev-list --count HEAD --not --remotes=origin 2>/dev/null)"
      unpushed_label="not on any origin branch (no upstream)"
    fi
  fi
  if [[ "$dirty" == <-> && "$unpushed" == <-> ]]; then
    if (( dirty == 0 && unpushed == 0 )); then
      work="clean (0 uncommitted, 0 $unpushed_label)"
    else
      work="$dirty uncommitted path(s), $unpushed commit(s) $unpushed_label"
      at_risk=1
    fi
  elif [[ "$dirty" == <-> ]]; then
    work="$dirty uncommitted path(s); commit count unavailable (git rev-list failed)"
    at_risk=1
  else
    work="unknown — worktree could not be inspected"
    at_risk=1
  fi
  _hspawn_say work "$work"
  _hspawn_say repo "$repo"
  _hspawn_say created "${created_at:-?}${creator:+ by session $creator}"
  if [[ -n "$closed_at" ]]; then
    _hspawn_say closed "pane closed by hreap --close at $closed_at (${closed_by:-hreap}); finishing that teardown"
  fi
  if [[ "$agent_status" == "working" ]]; then
    _hspawn_say warn "the agent in $pane is still working; removal kills it" >&2
  fi

  local question="Remove workspace $ws and its worktree ($work)?"
  if [[ -z "$ws_live" ]]; then
    if [[ -n "$wt_path" && -d "$wt_path" ]]; then
      question="Workspace $ws is gone; remove its worktree and registry entry ($work)?"
    else
      question="Workspace $ws is gone and no worktree dir is on disk; drop the registry entry?"
    fi
  fi
  if (( ! yes )); then
    if (( $+functions[confirm] )); then
      confirm "$question" || { echo "aborted"; return 1; }
    else
      read -q "?$question [y/N] " || { echo; echo "aborted"; return 1; }
      echo
    fi
  elif (( at_risk && ! force )) && [[ -n "$ws_live" || ( -n "$wt_path" && -d "$wt_path" ) ]]; then
    # The guard holds on BOTH paths: a gone workspace whose worktree dir is
    # still on disk (exactly what `hreap --close` leaves behind) has the same
    # work to lose. Only with no dir left is --yes free to drop the entry.
    echo "Error: refusing --yes: $work — removing the worktree would discard it. Entry kept: $registry" >&2
    echo "       Commit/push first, run without --yes to decide interactively, or add --force." >&2
    return 1
  fi

  if [[ -n "$ws_live" ]]; then
    local reply
    if ! reply="$(herdr worktree remove --workspace "$ws" --force 2>&1)"; then
      echo "Error: herdr worktree remove failed (registry entry kept: $registry):" >&2
      printf '%s\n' "$reply" >&2
      return 1
    fi
    _hspawn_say removed "workspace $ws${wt_path:+ + worktree $wt_path}"
  else
    _hspawn_say note "workspace $ws is gone already; finishing the teardown herdr no longer can"
    if [[ -n "$wt_path" && -d "$wt_path" ]]; then
      # git refuses a dirty worktree without --force; discarding is exactly
      # what was just confirmed (interactively, or via --yes --force).
      local wr_force="" wr_out
      (( at_risk || force )) && wr_force="--force"
      if ! wr_out="$(command git -C "$repo" worktree remove ${wr_force:+"$wr_force"} "$wt_path" 2>&1)"; then
        echo "Error: git -C ${(q)repo} worktree remove${wr_force:+ $wr_force} ${(q)wt_path} failed (registry entry kept: $registry):" >&2
        printf '%s\n' "$wr_out" >&2
        return 1
      fi
      _hspawn_say removed "worktree $wt_path (git worktree remove${wr_force:+ $wr_force})"
    else
      _hspawn_say note "no worktree dir on disk${wt_path:+ at $wt_path} — nothing to remove"
    fi
  fi
  rm -f -- "$registry"
  _hspawn_say registry "removed $registry"
  _hspawn_say branch "$branch — hdespawn never deletes branches; if it is still there: git -C ${(q)repo} branch -D ${(q)branch}"
}

#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hreap — every Claude process herdr hosts, detected or not; reap the tagged idle
#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `herdr agent list` shows only what herdr *detected*. herdmates teammates and
# panes stuck on the trust dialog are absent from it while still holding memory
# (2026-08-30: 15 teammates idle 3h+ = 4.5 GB, invisible). hreap walks every
# pane in `herdr api snapshot`, asks `herdr pane process-info` for the pane's
# foreground processes, and keeps the ones that are Claude: argv[0] basename
# `claude` (leads, `clauth start` children) or a path under `/claude/versions/`
# (the versioned binary Claude uses for teammates, which herdr does not
# recognise — its process name is the bare version, e.g. `2.1.251`).
#
# Idle age: the snapshot has no timestamps, so it is now − mtime of the session
# transcript (~/.claude/projects/<cwd with / and . as ->/<session>.jsonl; if that
# guess misses, a glob over every project dir there and under
# ~/.clauth/profiles/*/runtime-*/projects/). Claude appends to the transcript
# while it works, so "idle" means idle. With no transcript at all the process
# age from `ps -o etimes` is shown instead, marked `*` — an upper bound.
# Memory: VmRSS + VmSwap of the claude process itself, from /proc. Its MCP
# servers and tool shells are not counted, so the true cost is higher.
#
# --close is the only writing mode and it is deliberately narrow: it closes
# panes that are tagged in the hspawn registry AND whose agent status is idle
# or done AND that are idle (transcript-based) for at least --older minutes,
# via `herdr pane close`. Any other status — working, blocked, unknown (an
# undetected teammate or trust-dialog pane) — is skipped with a `skip:` line;
# there is deliberately NO override flag, because a transcript can sit
# untouched through a 45-minute tool call while the agent works: killing a
# working agent takes an explicit raw `herdr pane close <pane>`. Mind the
# blast radius: bare --close is MACHINE-WIDE — it acts on every registry-
# tagged pane meeting those tests, whoever spawned it (the user at a keyboard
# included); --mine is what narrows it to this session's own spawns, and the
# docs should only ever show the --mine form. The registry entry is NOT
# deleted — it is annotated with closed_at/closed_by so `hdespawn <slug>` can
# finish the teardown later (worktree, workspace if still open, entry) behind
# its uncommitted/unpushed guard; each per-close line names that command. An
# entry carrying closed_at no longer tags a live pane (a new pane could reuse
# the id), so closed spawns leave TAGGED, --mine and --close on the next run.
# Untagged panes — other people's sessions, teams, anything hspawn did not
# create — are never touched, whatever their age.
_hreap_fmt_dur() {
  local s="$1"
  if [[ "$s" != <-> ]]; then
    print -r -- "?"
  elif (( s < 60 )); then
    print -r -- "<1m"
  elif (( s < 3600 )); then
    print -r -- "$(( s / 60 ))m"
  elif (( s < 86400 )); then
    printf '%dh%02dm\n' $(( s / 3600 )) $(( (s % 3600) / 60 ))
  else
    printf '%dd%02dh\n' $(( s / 86400 )) $(( (s % 86400) / 3600 ))
  fi
}

_hreap_fmt_kb() {
  local kb="$1"
  if [[ "$kb" != <-> ]]; then
    print -r -- "?"
  elif (( kb < 1024 )); then
    print -r -- "${kb}K"
  elif (( kb < 1048576 )); then
    print -r -- "$(( kb / 1024 ))M"
  else
    printf '%.1fG\n' $(( kb / 1048576.0 ))
  fi
}

hreap() {
  local close=0 mine=0 json=0 older=30
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      --close) close=1 ;;
      --mine)  mine=1 ;;
      --json)  json=1 ;;
      --older|--older=*)
        if [[ "$1" == --older ]]; then
          older="${2-}"
          shift
        else
          older="${1#*=}"
        fi
        if [[ "$older" != <-> ]]; then
          echo "Error: --older needs a whole number of minutes (hreap --help)" >&2
          return 1
        fi ;;
      -h|--help)
        printf '%s\n' \
          'Usage: hreap [--close] [--mine] [--older <minutes>] [--json]' \
          '  Every Claude process in a herdr pane, detected or not, oldest-idle first.' \
          '  --older N   only rows idle >= N minutes (default 30; 0 = everything)' \
          '  --mine      only hspawn spawns created by this Claude session ($CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID)' \
          '  --close     close the listed panes that are hspawn-tagged, agent-status idle/done, AND' \
          '              idle >= N (transcript-based). working/blocked/unknown agents are skipped, with' \
          '              no override — killing a working agent takes a raw `herdr pane close`. Untagged' \
          '              panes are never touched. Read-only without --close.' \
          '              WARNING: without --mine, --close is machine-wide — every tagged pane on this' \
          '              box, whoever spawned it, not just yours.' \
          '  --json      machine-readable rows; read-only, refuses to combine with --close' \
          '  idle marked * = no transcript found, process age shown instead' >&2
        return 0 ;;
      *)
        echo "Error: unknown argument '$1' (hreap --help)" >&2
        return 1 ;;
    esac
    shift
  done
  if (( json && close )); then
    echo "Error: --json is read-only — it prints the rows and returns before anything is" >&2
    echo "       closed, so --json --close would silently reap nothing. Run one or the other." >&2
    return 1
  fi
  if ! command -v jq &>/dev/null; then
    echo "Error: hreap needs jq to read herdr's JSON replies" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  if (( mine )) && [[ -z "$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID" ]]; then
    echo "Error: --mine needs \$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID (run it from inside a Claude session)" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  zmodload -F zsh/stat b:zstat 2>/dev/null
  zmodload zsh/datetime 2>/dev/null

  local snapshot
  snapshot="$(herdr api snapshot)" || return 1
  local state_dir now
  state_dir="$(_hspawn_state_dir)"
  now="${EPOCHSECONDS:-$(command date +%s)}"
  # Field separator for the jq -> read hand-offs (jq writes it as "\u001f").
  # IFS whitespace, tab included, collapses runs and would shift empty fields;
  # a control character does not.
  local sep=$'\x1f'

  local -a rows=() cands=()
  # $total is offered as the full census ("of N in herdr panes"), so a pane it
  # could not ask about has to be visible rather than folded into silence: a
  # failed `pane process-info` used to `continue` before counting, and the
  # census then read lower than the truth with nothing saying why — the same
  # shape of silent undercount that made hreap necessary in the first place.
  local total=0 unqueried=0
  local pane ws detected astatus sid disp aname wslabel wpath
  local pinfo pid pname pcwd dashed tfile mtime idle_s idle_src et
  local rss_kb swap_kb mem_kb k v registry reg_vals tagged creator rname rslug rbranch rclosed name
  while IFS="$sep" read -r pane ws detected astatus sid disp aname wslabel wpath; do
    pinfo="$(herdr pane process-info --pane "$pane" 2>/dev/null)" || { (( unqueried++ )); continue; }
    while IFS="$sep" read -r pid pname pcwd; do
      [[ -n "$pid" ]] || continue
      (( total++ ))

      # Idle: transcript mtime, else process age.
      idle_s="" idle_src="" tfile=""
      if [[ -n "$sid" ]]; then
        dashed="${pcwd//[\/.]/-}"
        cands=( "$HOME/.claude/projects/$dashed/$sid.jsonl"(N) )
        (( ${#cands} )) || cands=( "$HOME"/.claude/projects/*/"$sid.jsonl"(N) \
                                   "$HOME"/.clauth/profiles/*/runtime-*/projects/*/"$sid.jsonl"(N) )
        if (( ${#cands} )); then
          tfile="${cands[1]}"
          if (( $+builtins[zstat] )); then
            mtime="$(zstat +mtime "$tfile" 2>/dev/null)"
          else
            mtime="$(command stat -c %Y "$tfile" 2>/dev/null)"
          fi
          if [[ "$mtime" == <-> ]]; then
            idle_s=$(( now - mtime ))
            idle_src="transcript"
          fi
        fi
      fi
      if [[ -z "$idle_s" ]]; then
        et="$(command ps -o etimes= -p "$pid" 2>/dev/null)"
        et="${et// /}"
        if [[ "$et" == <-> ]]; then
          idle_s="$et"
          idle_src="process"
        fi
      fi

      # Memory: RSS + swap of this pid.
      rss_kb=0 swap_kb=0 mem_kb=""
      if [[ -r "/proc/$pid/status" ]]; then
        while read -r k v _; do
          case "$k" in
            VmRSS:)  rss_kb="$v" ;;
            VmSwap:) swap_kb="$v" ;;
          esac
        done < "/proc/$pid/status"
        mem_kb=$(( rss_kb + swap_kb ))
      fi

      # hspawn registry tag. @sh quotes strings only, so every field goes
      # through tostring (a JSON array/number would otherwise become extra
      # shell words under eval). jq -e fails on a corrupt or 0-byte file, and
      # that MUST leave tagged=false: an entry hreap cannot read entitles
      # --close to nothing (it used to leave tagged=true with an empty
      # creator — still eligible for bare --close).
      registry="$state_dir/${pane//:/_}.json"
      tagged=false creator="" rname="" rslug="" rbranch="" rclosed=""
      if [[ -f "$registry" ]] && reg_vals="$(jq -re '@sh "creator=\(.creator_session // "" | tostring) rname=\(.name // "" | tostring) rslug=\(.slug // "" | tostring) rbranch=\(.branch // "" | tostring) rclosed=\(.closed_at // "" | tostring)"' "$registry" 2>/dev/null)"; then
        eval "$reg_vals"
        if [[ -n "$rclosed" ]]; then
          # Already closed by hreap: that pane is gone, so a live process under
          # the same id belongs to someone else. Not tagged, not attributed.
          creator="" rname="" rslug="" rbranch=""
        else
          tagged=true
        fi
      fi
      if (( mine )) && [[ "$tagged" != true || "$creator" != "$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID" ]]; then
        continue
      fi
      if (( older > 0 )); then
        [[ -n "$idle_s" ]] || continue          # unknown idle never passes a threshold
        (( idle_s >= older * 60 )) || continue
      fi

      # Name: detected agent name, else the pane's own terminal title, else the
      # registry name. herdr 0.8.2 panes carry NO display_agent/label/title — not
      # in `api snapshot`, not in `pane get` (whose keys are agent, agent_session,
      # agent_status, cwd, focused, foreground_cwd, pane_id, revision, scroll,
      # tab_id, terminal_id, terminal_title, terminal_title_stripped, tokens,
      # workspace_id). A fallback chain on those three therefore always fell
      # through to "-" — live, 4 of 5 rows — and the `pane get` call that repeated
      # them cost one RPC per unnamed pane for a guaranteed-empty answer.
      # terminal_title_stripped is in the snapshot already, so $disp carries it
      # and no second call is needed.
      name="$aname"
      [[ -n "$name" ]] || name="$disp"
      [[ -n "$name" ]] || name="$rname"
      [[ -n "$name" ]] || name="-"

      rows+=("$(jq -n \
        --arg pane "$pane" --arg workspace "$ws" --arg workspace_label "$wslabel" \
        --arg worktree_path "$wpath" --arg name "$name" --arg status "$astatus" \
        --argjson detected "$([[ "$detected" == yes ]] && echo true || echo false)" \
        --argjson idle_s "${idle_s:-null}" --arg idle_src "$idle_src" --arg transcript "$tfile" \
        --argjson pid "$pid" --arg process "$pname" --arg cwd "$pcwd" --arg session "$sid" \
        --argjson rss_kb "$rss_kb" --argjson swap_kb "$swap_kb" --argjson mem_kb "${mem_kb:-null}" \
        --argjson tagged "$tagged" --arg creator "$creator" --arg slug "$rslug" --arg branch "$rbranch" \
        '$ARGS.named')")
    done < <(printf '%s' "$pinfo" | jq -r '
      .result.process_info.foreground_processes // [] | .[]
      | ((.argv // [])[0] // .argv0 // .name // "") as $a
      | select(($a | split("/") | last) == "claude" or ($a | test("/claude/versions/")) or .name == "claude")
      | [ (.pid | tostring), .name, (.cwd // "") ]
      | map(gsub("[\n\r]"; " ")) | join("\u001f")')
  done < <(printf '%s' "$snapshot" | jq -r '
    .result.snapshot as $s
    | ($s.agents // [] | map({key: .pane_id, value: (.name // "")}) | from_entries) as $names
    | ($s.workspaces // [] | map({key: .workspace_id,
        value: {label: (.label // ""), path: (.worktree.checkout_path // "")}}) | from_entries) as $wsi
    | $s.panes[]
    | [ .pane_id, .workspace_id,
        (if (.agent // "") != "" then "yes" else "no" end),
        (.agent_status // "unknown"),
        (.agent_session.value // ""),
        (.terminal_title_stripped // ""),
        ($names[.pane_id] // ""),
        ($wsi[.workspace_id].label // ""),
        ($wsi[.workspace_id].path // "") ]
    | map(tostring | gsub("[\n\r]"; " ")) | join("\u001f")')

  local sorted shown
  sorted="$(printf '%s\n' "${rows[@]}" | jq -s 'sort_by(.idle_s // -1) | reverse')"
  if (( json )); then
    printf '%s\n' "$sorted"
    return 0
  fi

  local filter="idle >= ${older}m"
  (( mine )) && filter+=", --mine"
  local unq_note=""
  (( unqueried )) && unq_note=" + $unqueried pane(s) that could not be queried"
  shown="$(printf '%s' "$sorted" | jq 'length')"
  if (( shown == 0 )); then
    echo "hreap: nothing matches ($filter) — $total Claude process(es) in herdr panes$unq_note"
    return 0
  fi

  local idle_h mem_h tag_h wsh starred=0
  printf '%-8s %-18s %-22s %-4s %-8s %-8s %-8s %s\n' PANE WORKSPACE NAME DET STATUS IDLE MEM TAGGED
  while IFS="$sep" read -r pane ws wslabel name detected astatus idle_s idle_src mem_kb tagged creator; do
    idle_h="$(_hreap_fmt_dur "$idle_s")"
    if [[ "$idle_src" == process ]]; then
      idle_h="${idle_h}*"
      starred=1
    fi
    mem_h="$(_hreap_fmt_kb "$mem_kb")"
    if [[ "$tagged" == true ]]; then
      tag_h="${creator:0:8}"
      [[ -n "$tag_h" ]] || tag_h="hspawn"
    else
      tag_h="-"
    fi
    wsh="$ws"
    [[ -n "$wslabel" ]] && wsh="$ws ($wslabel)"
    printf '%-8s %-18.18s %-22.22s %-4s %-8s %-8s %-8s %s\n' \
      "$pane" "$wsh" "$name" "$detected" "$astatus" "$idle_h" "$mem_h" "$tag_h"
  done < <(printf '%s' "$sorted" | jq -r '.[]
    | [ .pane, .workspace, .workspace_label, .name, (if .detected then "yes" else "no" end),
        .status, (.idle_s // ""), .idle_src, (.mem_kb // ""), .tagged, .creator ]
    | map(tostring) | join("\u001f")')
  local sum_kb
  sum_kb="$(printf '%s' "$sorted" | jq '[.[] | .mem_kb // 0] | add // 0')"
  echo "totals:    $shown Claude process(es) shown, $(_hreap_fmt_kb "$sum_kb") RSS+swap (of $total in herdr panes$unq_note; $filter)"
  (( starred )) && echo "           * no transcript found — process age shown, not idle time"

  if (( close )); then
    local closed=0 freed_kb=0 untagged=0 reply now_status
    local -a done_panes=()
    while IFS="$sep" read -r pane name astatus idle_s idle_src mem_kb tagged wpath rbranch rslug; do
      if [[ "$tagged" != true ]]; then
        (( untagged++ ))
        continue
      fi
      # Only an agent herdr reports as idle or done may be closed. working
      # means exactly that; blocked/unknown means a dialog or an undetected
      # process — none of them fair game, and there is no override flag.
      case "$astatus" in
        idle|done) ;;
        *)
          echo "skip:      $pane ($name) — status $astatus, not closing"
          continue ;;
      esac
      if [[ "$idle_src" != transcript ]]; then
        echo "skip:      $pane ($name) — tagged, but no transcript found; not closing on process age alone"
        continue
      fi
      (( idle_s >= older * 60 )) || continue
      [[ " ${done_panes[*]} " == *" $pane "* ]] && continue
      # $astatus came from the snapshot taken at the top of this run. Re-read it
      # here: there is deliberately no override flag, so "never close a working
      # agent" is the whole safety story and it has to hold at the moment of the
      # close, not at scan time. The scan is fast (~0.1 s over 9 panes on this
      # box), so the window is small — but it is not zero, and an agent that woke
      # inside it would be killed mid-turn.
      now_status="$(herdr pane get "$pane" 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.result.pane.agent_status // "unknown"')"
      # jq prints NOTHING on empty input, so that `// "unknown"` never fires when
      # the call itself fails or returns nothing — and the skip line below then
      # read "status changed to  since the scan", sending whoever reads it after a
      # status change that never happened. The skip is right either way; only the
      # stated reason was wrong. Unknown is also the correct value: an agent herdr
      # will not talk about is exactly what "unknown" means here.
      [[ -n "$now_status" ]] || now_status="unknown"
      case "$now_status" in
        idle|done) ;;
        *)
          echo "skip:      $pane ($name) — status changed to $now_status since the scan, not closing"
          continue ;;
      esac
      if reply="$(herdr pane close "$pane" 2>&1)"; then
        done_panes+=("$pane")
        (( closed++ ))
        [[ "$mem_kb" == <-> ]] && (( freed_kb += mem_kb ))
        # Annotate, never delete: hdespawn finishes the teardown from this entry.
        registry="$state_dir/${pane//:/_}.json"
        if jq --arg closed_at "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" \
              --arg closed_by "${CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID:-hreap}" \
              '. + {closed_at: $closed_at, closed_by: $closed_by}' "$registry" > "$registry.tmp" 2>/dev/null \
           && command mv -f -- "$registry.tmp" "$registry"; then
          :
        else
          command rm -f -- "$registry.tmp"
          echo "warn:      could not annotate $registry — hdespawn will still find it by slug" >&2
        fi
        echo "closed:    $pane ($name, idle $(_hreap_fmt_dur "$idle_s"), $(_hreap_fmt_kb "$mem_kb"))"
        # The PANE ID, not the slug. The registry file is keyed on the pane id,
        # so `hdespawn wN:p1` is an exact file lookup, while `hdespawn <slug>`
        # SCANS every entry and refuses outright when two match. This line runs
        # only where that collision is being manufactured: --close deliberately
        # keeps the entry so hdespawn can finish the teardown, so a second spawn
        # of the same slug — a retried ticket, the same branch respawned — makes
        # the printed command fail on the entry it just named. The slug is still
        # shown, as text, for the human reading the line.
        echo "           worktree kept${wpath:+: $wpath}${rbranch:+ ($rbranch)} — may hold uncommitted work. Finish with: hdespawn $pane${rslug:+  (slug: $rslug)}"
      else
        echo "Error: herdr pane close $pane failed: $reply" >&2
      fi
    done < <(printf '%s' "$sorted" | jq -r '.[]
      | [ .pane, .name, .status, (.idle_s // ""), .idle_src, (.mem_kb // ""), .tagged, .worktree_path, .branch, .slug ]
      | map(tostring) | join("\u001f")')
    echo "reaped:    $closed pane(s), ~$(_hreap_fmt_kb "$freed_kb") RSS+swap; $untagged untagged row(s) left alone"
    if (( closed )); then
      echo "           registry entries annotated (closed_at), not removed — the hdespawn command(s) above finish the teardown"
    fi
  fi
}

#==============================================================================
# herdr-help — in-shell cheat sheet
#==============================================================================
# Moved here from zsh/zshrc.help (DO-563). It had been sitting in the general
# help module, which the FULL install sources and a modular adopter never does
# — so the one command that lists hspawn/hreap/clauth was missing on exactly
# the machines whose owner had not read the guide, and the write-up had to
# apologise for its absence. `dothelp` still lists it; the definition lives
# with the rest of the layer it documents.
# Herdr agent-workspace help
herdr-help() {
  cat << 'EOF'
🐑 Herdr Agent Workspace Quick Reference

SPAWN & REAP:
  hspawn   - worktree + branch + agent + first prompt, in one call
             hspawn [-p profile] [-m model] [-e effort] [--mode M] [-b ref]
                    [-n name] <repo-dir> <slug> [prompt...]
  hreap    - census of EVERY claude process in a herdr pane, detected or not
             hreap                                 DEFAULTS to idle >= 30 min
             hreap --older 0                       every process, no idle filter
             hreap [--older N] [--mine] [--json]   read-only
             hreap --close --mine                  close only this session's spawns
  hdespawn - tear one spawn down: pane, workspace, worktree, registry entry
             hdespawn [-y|--yes] [-f|--force] <slug|pane-id>
  Registry:  ${HSPAWN_STATE_DIR:-${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/hspawn}

ACCOUNTS (every session on its own credential file):
  claude                  - isolated onto the least-used clauth profile, AND a
                            team lead. Prints which account it took.
  claude-as <profile>     - the same, on a named account
  CLAUDE_ISOLATION_OFF=1  - opt out: run on the shared global credential
  clauth list             - profiles with usage
  clauth login <name>     - add an account; more accounts = smaller blast radius
  clauth start <profile>  - supervised worker (--with-fallback quota rotation),
                            launches the BINARY so it is NOT a team lead
  clauth <profile>        - switches the GLOBAL credential, which nothing reads
                            once sessions are isolated. AVOID: it overwrites that
                            file under every holder at once
  claude-doctor           - auth + MCP health; RUN BEFORE 'clauth <profile>'
                            (a switch restores clauth's STORED tokens over the
                             live ones — if they have drifted it can log out
                             every session holding that profile)

PLUGINS:
  herdr-lazy check          - installed vs declared in plugins.list
  herdr-lazy sync           - install what plugins.list declares and is missing
  herdr-lazy sync --prune   - that, AND remove anything installed but undeclared
  herdr plugin action list  - verify an action id before binding it

RESOURCES:
  build-limits - active build/test parallelism tier
                 (tight inside a herdr pane, loose outside)

SETUP (both fail silently if skipped):
  ~/.dotfiles/scripts/herdr-claude-wire.sh
                                         - statusLine + agent skill file; the
                                           two things ./install cannot do
VERIFY:
  ~/.dotfiles/scripts/verify-tools.sh --herdr
                                         - server env, unit enablement, plugin
                                           deps on the SERVER's PATH, wiring.
                                           Drop --herdr only if you installed
                                           the whole repo, not just herdr.
  ~/.dotfiles/scripts/herdr-keyprobe.sh  - what bytes a chord actually produces
  herdr agent explain <pane>             - why a pane is (not) detected
  herdr server reload-config | jq '.result.status'
                                         - must say 'applied', never 'partial'

⚠️  hreap --close without --mine is MACHINE-WIDE, not just your spawns
⚠️  Never start or restart the herdr server from inside a pane or a Claude
    session. Use: systemctl --user restart herdr-server.service
⚠️  The server's PATH is a snapshot from when it started; a newly installed
    tool stays invisible to plugins and popups until it restarts

Full guide: ~/.dotfiles/docs/HERDR_GUIDE.md
EOF
}
